UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because methionine and cysteine are the two standard amino acids whose side chains contain sulfur. Methionine is a nonpolar, hydrophobic amino acid whose side chain terminates in a thioether linkage, while cysteine carries a sulfhydryl \(-\text{SH}\) (thiol) group. Of the two, cysteine's thiol is the redox-active one that is readily oxidized; it is a potent nucleophile that often forms a covalent disulfide bond with another cysteine. (Choice A) is incorrect because cysteine does contain sulfur, but serine is the oxygen analog — its side chain is the hydroxyl \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\). Among serine, threonine, and cysteine, serine bears a hydroxyl group rather than a sulfhydryl. (Choice B) is correct: both methionine (thioether) and cysteine (thiol) carry sulfur in the R-group. (Choice C) is incorrect because methionine does contain sulfur, but threonine is a hydroxyl-containing (\(-\text{OH}\)) polar amino acid, not a sulfur-containing one. (Choice D) is incorrect because cysteine contains sulfur, but selenocysteine is not one of the 20 standard amino acids — it is a rare non-standard residue that occasionally appears in proteins, found in Archaea, eubacteria, and animals. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which standard amino acid side chains contain sulfur.$px$ WHERE id = '003b2724-a513-4ee3-a862-24176f05452d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This item addresses the mole concept and molar mass. The quantity \(6.02 \times 10^{23}\) atoms is one mole, and the molar mass of an element in grams per mole is numerically equal to its atomic weight in atomic mass units. One mole of phosphorus therefore has a mass of \(30.97\ \text{g}\) and one mole of sulfur \(32.07\ \text{g}\); summing gives \(63.04\ \text{g}\), closest to \(63.0\ \text{g}\). (Choice B) The numerical sum \(63.0\) is correct, but atomic mass units quantify the mass of a single atom, not a mole of atoms; the mass of one mole of an element is expressed in grams, so labeling a macroscopic sample in amu is a unit mismatch. (Choice C) The value \(31.5\ \text{g}\) is the average of the two atomic weights rather than their sum, which would apply only to a single mole total rather than one mole of each element. (Choice D) The value \(1.05 \times 10^{-22}\ \text{g}\) is the mass of one pair of atoms—the molar mass divided by Avogadro's number—rather than the mass of an entire mole of each element. Skill 2: this requires applying the equivalence between atomic weight and molar mass to compute a sample mass with correct units.$px$ WHERE id = '0149dbef-f92f-4c20-bbbf-63ad2493f06f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.' In a myelinated axon, voltage-gated Na+ channels are clustered only at the nodes of Ranvier; between nodes the myelin acts as insulation so that the inward Na+ current spreads passively down the internode and still arrives above threshold at the next node, producing fast saltatory ("leaping") conduction. When myelin insulation is compromised, electrical signaling becomes slower, which is why demyelinating diseases like MS impair conduction even when the axon and its channels survive. (Choice A) is correct: myelin's job is to keep the internodal membrane sealed so charge does not dissipate before reaching the next node; stripping it lets local current leak out, the depolarization falls off before the next node, and the axon reverts toward slow continuous conduction. (Choice B) is incorrect: myelin is glial-derived insulation, not a metabolic fuel source, and the axon's mitochondria and Na+/K+ pumps are unaffected by demyelination, so ATP supply and repolarization are not the bottleneck. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem specifies the voltage-gated Na+ channels remain intact, and it is the loss of insulation—not loss of channels—that slows the signal; the channels are still present and can still fire at the nodes. (Choice D) is incorrect: myelin does not set the direction of propagation; the action potential still travels toward the axon terminals because the membrane behind it is refractory, regardless of whether myelin is present. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the insulating role of myelin in saltatory conduction to a novel demyelination scenario and predict the biophysical cause of slowed signaling.$px$ WHERE id = '01b37a48-8ac2-4952-9962-20344ef1677b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.' The photon emitted in a downward transition carries the energy difference between the two levels, \(\Delta E = E_\text{high} - E_\text{low}\), and by \(E = hc/\lambda\) the shortest-wavelength photon is the one with the largest \(|\Delta E|\). Because \(E_n = -k/n^2\), the levels are packed together near the top and spread far apart near the bottom, so the size of a gap depends far more on how low the transition lands than on how many levels it spans. Evaluating \(-k/n^2\): \(E_1 = -k\), \(E_2 = -k/4\), \(E_3 = -k/9\), \(E_4 = -k/16\), \(E_6 = -k/36\). (Choice A) is correct: \(n = 4 \to n = 1\) spans from \(-k/16\) down to \(-k\), a gap of \(0.9375k\) — the largest of the four because it ends in the deep \(n = 1\) level while starting high enough to add most of the remaining span, giving the highest-energy, shortest-wavelength photon. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(n = 6 \to n = 2\) crosses the most levels (four steps), which tempts students who equate a bigger jump in \(n\) with more energy, but it runs from \(-k/36\) only down to \(-k/4\), a gap of just \(0.222k\) — the smallest here and the longest wavelength — because the upper levels are crowded and it never reaches \(n = 1\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(n = 3 \to n = 1\) also lands in the ground level and is close, but its gap is \(k - k/9 = 0.889k\), slightly less than \(n = 4 \to n = 1\); starting one level lower than \(n = 4\) leaves out a small but decisive slice of energy. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(n = 2 \to n = 1\) ends in the ground level too, yet with a gap of only \(k - k/4 = 0.750k\) it is the smallest of the three transitions terminating at \(n = 1\), so its photon is not the shortest wavelength. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the \(E_n = -k/n^2\) energy-level formula to rank transition gaps and connect the largest gap to the shortest-wavelength photon, rather than recognize a stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = '02035f8c-0da5-4b7c-905c-96564d538805';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because arrow X (reactant level to the peak) represents the activation energy \(E_a\), while arrow Y (reactant level to product level) represents the overall \(\Delta G\). An enzyme is a catalyst that lowers the activation energy by stabilizing the transition state, so for the reaction to be catalyzed the activation energy in the presence of the enzyme must be smaller than the activation energy required in the absence of the enzyme; thus arrow X shortens. Enzymes do not affect the thermodynamics of reactions: for a reversible reaction the equilibrium constant \(K_{eq}\) is unchanged, and only the rate at which equilibrium is reached is altered. Because \(\Delta G\) is a thermodynamic quantity set only by the reactant and product energy levels, arrow Y is unchanged. (Choice A) reverses the assignment: it shrinks the \(\Delta G\) arrow and leaves \(E_a\) untouched, but enzymes lower \(E_a\), not \(\Delta G\). (Choice B) is correct: the enzyme lowers \(E_a\) (arrow X) while leaving \(\Delta G\) (arrow Y) unchanged. (Choice C) is a partial truth — the enzyme does lower the energy of the barrier, but it does NOT lower \(\Delta G\); the diagram's reactant and product levels are fixed, so the two arrows cannot shrink equally. (Choice D) correctly lowers the barrier (arrow X) but wrongly claims the enzyme also drives the product level lower, lengthening arrow Y; the reactant and product free-energy levels that fix \(\Delta G\) are unchanged, so arrow Y neither lengthens nor shortens. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that catalysts lower \(E_a\) but not \(\Delta G\) to interpret which labeled arrow on the energy diagram changes.$px$ WHERE id = '02092cae-b064-4596-85a9-08d8df9c4880';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' Epithelia are classified by cell shape and the number of cell layers, and each arrangement suits a particular function: a single (simple) layer of flat (squamous) cells presents the thinnest possible barrier, whereas stacking cells into multiple (stratified) layers or building taller cell shapes increases barrier thickness at the cost of easy diffusion. The task is to match a functional demand — the shortest diffusion path — to the epithelial structure that provides it, rather than to identify a tissue from its name. (Choice A) is correct: simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of flat cells, and the text notes these cells "facilitate diffusion in tissues, such as the areas of gas exchange in the lungs"; because the cells are both thin (squamous) and one layer deep (simple), they minimize the distance gases must travel, which is exactly what the design requires. (Choice B) is incorrect: simple cuboidal cells are "cube-shaped with a single, central nucleus" and line kidney tubules and glands where they secrete and filter; their greater height makes a thicker barrier than flat squamous cells, so they are not optimized for rapid diffusion. (Choice C) is incorrect: pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the respiratory tract's conducting airways and uses cilia and goblet cells to move mucus, but its tall columnar cells form a comparatively thick barrier suited to protection and mucus transport, not gas exchange. (Choice D) is incorrect: stratified squamous epithelium consists of multiple layers and, as in the skin, provides "protection... from outside abrasion and damage"; the many layers deliberately lengthen the diffusion path, the opposite of what gas exchange needs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between epithelial structure (cell shape and layer number) and function (diffusion distance) to select the tissue best suited to a novel design goal.$px$ WHERE id = '031fda85-df68-441c-9382-17d53d88409e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because the principal disaccharide in milk is lactose, and complete hydrolysis of lactose yields galactose and glucose joined by a \(\beta(1\rightarrow4)\) glycosidic bond, written Gal(\(\beta1,4\))Glc. The intestinal enzyme lactase cleaves this bond and liberates one molecule of galactose and one of glucose for absorption.

(Choice A) is incorrect: glucose and fructose are the monomers of sucrose (table sugar), Glc(\(\alpha1,2\))Fru, not the sugar in milk. Fructose is not a component of the milk disaccharide.

(Choice B) is correct: the milk disaccharide lactose is composed of galactose linked to glucose, so hydrolysis releases exactly these two monosaccharides.

(Choice C) is incorrect: two glucose units joined by an \(\alpha(1\rightarrow4)\) bond form maltose, a starch/glycogen hydrolysis product, not the milk sugar; milk's disaccharide contains galactose, not a second glucose.

(Choice D) is incorrect: this pairing swaps in fructose for glucose. Galactose is one correct monomer, but the second monomer of the milk disaccharide is glucose, not fructose, so this pair is wrong.

As a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) item, this question asks you to recall the identity of the monosaccharide building blocks of a common disaccharide — a foundational fact about carbohydrate structure.$px$ WHERE id = '0351c1a0-32f1-4ba3-89f0-03e9ea2cb338';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the calculation must chain three steps — deduce the electron count \(n\) from the balanced cell, compute \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) by subtraction, then convert to \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) in kilojoules. First, identify the electrodes: the species with the larger reduction potential is reduced, so permanganate (\(+1.51\ \text{V}\)) is the cathode and the iron couple (\(+0.77\ \text{V}\)) is the anode, where \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) is oxidized to \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\). Although the iron half-reaction transfers only one electron per iron, the balanced overall reaction multiplies it by \(5\) to match the five electrons accepted by one permanganate; the number of electrons transferred per turnover of the balanced equation is therefore \(n = 5\), not \(1\). Next, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 1.51\ \text{V} - 0.77\ \text{V} = +0.74\ \text{V}\); the positive value confirms a spontaneous galvanic cell (\(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\)). Note that \(E^{\circ}\) is intensive and is never multiplied by the stoichiometric coefficient. Finally, \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -(5)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol})(0.74\ \text{V}) = -356{,}994.5\ \text{J} \approx -357{,}000\ \text{J} = -357\ \text{kJ}\). (Choice A) \(-1100\ \text{kJ}\) results from adding the two reduction potentials, \(1.51 + 0.77 = 2.28\ \text{V}\), instead of subtracting them; \(-(5)(96{,}485)(2.28) \approx -1.10\times10^{6}\ \text{J} = -1100\ \text{kJ}\) reflects the misconception that \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) is the sum of the half-cell potentials. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must extract the electron count from the balanced equation, apply the subtractive convention for \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), and carry the result through the \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) relationship with correct sign and units. (Choice B) \(-71\ \text{kJ}\) uses \(n = 1\), the electrons transferred per iron in the unscaled \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) half-reaction, rather than the \(n = 5\) electrons transferred per balanced reaction; \(-(1)(96{,}485)(0.74) = -71{,}400\ \text{J} = -71\ \text{kJ}\), undercounting the electrons by a factor of five. (Choice C) \(+357\ \text{kJ}\) has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign: it comes from computing the cell potential as \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} = 0.77 - 1.51 = -0.74\ \text{V}\), which yields \(\Delta G^{\circ} > 0\) and incorrectly labels a spontaneous galvanic reaction as nonspontaneous. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '0377b9df-65ad-4edc-b22e-c3b56cd6d918';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because under basic conditions the carbonyl of an aldose is deprotonated at the adjacent carbon to form a common \(C1\text{–}C2\) enediol intermediate. Because both C1 and C2 lose their fixed geometry in this planar enediol, re-protonation can occur in three ways: back to the original aldose, at C1 to give the corresponding 2-ketose, or at C2 from the opposite face to give the C2 epimer of the starting aldose. For D-glucose, the 2-ketose reached through this shared enediol is D-fructose, and the C2 epimer is D-mannose. All three therefore equilibrate through the same intermediate, so the chromatogram shows D-glucose, D-fructose, and D-mannose.

(Choice A) is incorrect: D-fructose is correctly included as the 2-ketose, but D-galactose differs from glucose at C4, not C2. The \(C1\text{–}C2\) enediol only scrambles the configuration at C2, so a C4 epimer cannot be produced by this pathway.

(Choice B) is incorrect: This set contains no ketose, yet the enediol mechanism necessarily passes through the 2-ketose (fructose) form; it also lists D-galactose, a C4 epimer that the C1–C2 enediol cannot generate. Epimerization here does not occur by a direct one-step swap of an aldose for another aldose.

(Choice D) is incorrect: D-fructose is a valid product, but stopping here ignores that the same enediol also allows re-protonation at C2 from the opposite face, generating the C2 epimer D-mannose. The interconversion is not limited to the aldose and its ketose.

(Choice C) is correct: This set correctly pairs the starting aldose with its 2-ketose (D-fructose) and its C2 epimer (D-mannose), the two products reachable through the shared C1–C2 enediol.

At the Skill 2 level, you are not recalling a named list of interconverting sugars; you must apply the enediol mechanism to a specific starting aldose, recognize that only the C1 and C2 positions can be altered, and deduce which particular ketose and epimer result.$px$ WHERE id = '03a9b67f-0ab3-42e1-abed-860f3e69efc3';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$At the sound of the starting gun, a sprinter's adrenal medulla releases a surge of epinephrine into her bloodstream — even before her leg muscles have begun any heavy contractile work. Apart from its well-known role in mobilizing fuel by activating glycogenolysis and lipolysis, this catecholamine also acts on beta-adrenergic receptors of the sympathetic "fight-or-flight" system to ready the body for the effort ahead. Reasoning from the beta-adrenergic actions of a catecholamine, which statement best explains how epinephrine prepares the sprinter's body for the physical exertion about to come?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because epinephrine is a catecholamine of the sympathetic fight-or-flight response, and its beta-adrenergic actions ready the body for exertion in two complementary ways. Acting on beta-1 receptors in the heart, it increases heart rate (positive chronotropy) and contractile force (positive inotropy); because cardiac output equals heart rate times stroke volume, both effects together raise cardiac output and deliver more oxygenated blood to the muscles each minute. Acting through beta-adrenergic stimulation more broadly, it also raises basal metabolic rate, increasing whole-body oxygen consumption and heat production (thermogenesis). Because catecholamines signal through fast cell-surface receptors and cAMP rather than slow gene transcription, these changes develop within seconds, well before the leg muscles begin heavy work.

(Choice B) is correct: raising heart rate and contractility increases cardiac output while a higher metabolic rate raises oxygen consumption and heat, so the cardiovascular and thermogenic arms of the sympathetic response prime the body for imminent work.
(Choice A) is incorrect: epinephrine drives the sympathetic, not the parasympathetic, response; it speeds and strengthens the heartbeat and raises metabolic rate rather than slowing the heart and conserving energy — the direction of every effect is reversed here.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly captures the rise in cardiac output but wrongly denies the thermogenic effect. Catecholamines raise basal metabolic rate, so oxygen consumption and heat production climb rather than staying unchanged.
(Choice D) is incorrect: epinephrine is a fast-acting amine that works through rapid beta-adrenergic cell-surface signaling, not a slow genomic mechanism. New contractile-protein synthesis would take hours and cannot explain a cardiovascular and metabolic response that appears within seconds of the starting gun.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by asking you to reason from the beta-adrenergic sympathetic role of a catecholamine to explain how epinephrine raises cardiac output and metabolic rate to prepare the body for physical exertion.$px$ WHERE id = '0426c1d1-42a4-4230-86ea-d336982c2fe3';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A purified protease shows maximal activity near pH 6. Its catalytic mechanism requires that a single active-site side chain, which has an apparent pKa of about 6, be in its deprotonated form. A researcher assays the enzyme in a well-buffered solution at pH 4 — a mild shift that does not unfold the protein. What is the most likely effect on catalytic rate, and why?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Key side chains necessary for catalysis must be in the correct protonation state. For example, a side chain with an apparent pKa of around 6 must be deprotonated for optimal activity of trypsin, which shows increases in activity with pH centered at pH 6. This follows the '+2 rule': when the pH is about 2 units below the pKa, the group is roughly 99% protonated, and when the pH is about 2 units above the pKa, it is roughly 99% deprotonated. Here the assay pH (4) is about 2 units below the residue's pKa (~6), so the side chain is driven into its protonated form — the opposite of the deprotonated state catalysis requires — and activity drops even without any unfolding. (Choice A) is correct: at pH 4 (2 units below the pKa of 6) the residue is largely protonated, which is not the deprotonated state the mechanism needs, so the rate falls. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the +2 rule — lowering the pH below the pKa protonates the group, it does not deprotonate it, so the stated reason contradicts the direction of the acid-base equilibrium even though the predicted drop happens to be right for the wrong reason. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly notes the residue is protonated at pH 4 but then inverts the mechanism's requirement — the stem specifies the deprotonated form is catalytically active, so protonation lowers, not raises, activity. (Choice D) is incorrect: pH does not act only on substrate binding; pH can alter catalysis (kcat) by changing the protonation state of catalytic side chains, so a shift that protonates an essential residue changes turnover directly, not merely binding. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the pH–pKa relationship (the +2 rule) to predict the protonation state of a catalytic residue at a given pH and connect that state to the enzyme's activity.$px$ WHERE id = '04380d86-f9f5-4e90-bfde-fbbbf1f9fc7c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is A because the glycosidic bond linking the two glucose units of maltose is an acetal, and cleaving an acetal back to its component pieces is a hydrolysis reaction — water is consumed as the nucleophile that breaks the linkage. Adding one molecule of water across the \(\text{Glc}(\alpha 1{\rightarrow}4)\text{Glc}\) linkage splits the disaccharide into its two constituent monosaccharides, yielding two molecules of free glucose. Because the linkage is between two identical hexoses, the only sugar product is glucose.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Cleavage by inorganic phosphate is phosphorolysis, not hydrolysis, and it produces a phosphorylated sugar (e.g., glucose-1-phosphate) rather than a second free sugar; this is how glycogen phosphorylase mobilizes glycogen in cells, but it is a distinct reaction that does not use water and is not the general route for breaking a glycosidic bond.

(Choice B) is incorrect: Removing water (a condensation/dehydration) is the direction that forms a glycosidic bond, not the direction that cleaves one — running it would build a larger linked product instead of liberating the monomers, so this reverses the required chemistry.

(Choice A) is correct: adding water (hydrolysis) cleaves the acetal linkage and regenerates the two free glucose monosaccharides.

(Choice D) is incorrect: A mild oxidizing agent acts on the exposed anomeric carbon (aldehyde) of a reducing sugar, converting it to a carboxylic acid; this redox chemistry modifies a monosaccharide but does nothing to sever the glycosidic bond holding the two units together, so no separation into free sugars occurs.

Skill 2 is assessed here because the stem never states what breaks a glycosidic bond; the test-taker must recognize the linkage as an acetal, recall that acetal cleavage requires the addition of water, and then predict both the reagent and the specific monosaccharide products for this particular disaccharide.$px$ WHERE id = '0467463e-ce6b-43a4-9544-168d6a29cfc3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Mechanisms of Development'. The answer is C because differentiation arises from selective gene expression, not from any change to the underlying DNA: every somatic cell keeps the full, identical genome, and specialization comes from each cell transcribing only the portion of that genome its function requires. The A&P text states that "all cells in the body, beginning with the fertilized egg, contain the same DNA" and that "each type of cell only 'reads' the portions of DNA that are relevant to its own function," while the Biology text adds that "each cell does not turn on, or express, the same set of genes." Because the neuron and hepatocyte share a sequence but express different gene subsets, they build different proteins. (Choice A) is wrong because somatic cells "generally" retain the complete DNA rather than deleting unused genes during differentiation. (Choice B) is wrong because the two cells share the same DNA sequence inherited from the fertilized egg; expression—not sequence—differs. (Choice C) is correct because the identical genome is differentially transcribed, yielding cell-type-specific proteins. (Choice D) is wrong because transcription factors "either promote or inhibit" transcription—they regulate gene reading without mutating the genes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using observed data (identical genome but divergent protein output) to infer the underlying mechanism of differentiation rather than simply recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '05a40e05-6581-4b21-a456-b970781c2be7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' Point mutations affect a single base pair; the most common are substitutions, in which one base is replaced by another, and these mutations can result in the replacement of one amino acid by another, which may alter the function of the protein. Sickle-cell hemoglobin is the classic example: at position six, glutamate is replaced by valine through a single base change (a point mutation), yielding a full-length chain with a single altered residue. You must match the observed data — a chain of normal length, unchanged everywhere except one residue swapped — to the mutation class that produces exactly that outcome. (Choice A) is incorrect because a silent substitution, usually occurring in the third base of a codon and often specifying the same amino acid, leaves the protein sequence unchanged; yet here glutamate is demonstrably replaced by valine, so the change cannot be silent. (Choice B) is incorrect because a single-base deletion alters the translational reading frame (a frameshift mutation), scrambling every codon downstream and usually yielding a nonfunctional protein — not a full-length chain differing at only one position. (Choice C) is incorrect because a nonsense substitution generates a stop codon that would terminate the protein early, producing a truncated chain, whereas the described beta chain is the normal full length of 147 residues. (Choice D) is correct: a missense substitution replaces one base, changing a single codon so it specifies a different amino acid (glutamate to valine) while leaving the codon count and reading frame intact — exactly the full-length, single-residue change observed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of silent, missense, nonsense, and frameshift mutations to infer, from the length and residue pattern of the protein, which DNA-level event occurred.$px$ WHERE id = '06229ac9-f2e7-47f6-8940-75f41346e588';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher studies a metabolic enzyme that is inactive until a specific cellular stimulus appears. Once the stimulus activates the enzyme, its catalytic activity persists even after the researcher removes the stimulus, washes away all small molecules and ATP, and returns the cell to resting conditions — the enzyme cannot be switched back to its inactive state by the cell's normal machinery. Which regulatory mechanism best accounts for this behavior?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Regulatory mechanisms differ in a decisive property: whether they can be reversed by the cell. Reversible mechanisms include protein phosphorylation, the addition of a phosphoryl group that can be undone through the opposing actions of kinases and phosphatases; an irreversible mechanism is zymogen activation, in which inactive precursors become active through limited proteolysis by permanent peptide-bond cleavage. The key here is that the enzyme stays active after every stimulus and small molecule is removed, so only an irreversible mechanism fits. (Choice C) is correct: an inactive precursor (zymogen) is activated by limited proteolysis — peptide bonds are hydrolytically cleaved and cannot be re-formed by the cell, so once cut the enzyme remains active regardless of whether the original stimulus is present, exactly the persistence described. (Choice A) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that any covalent activation is permanent; phosphorylation is reversible, and a phosphatase would remove the phosphoryl group and switch the enzyme off once the activating signal disappears, so activity would not persist. (Choice B) is incorrect: allosteric activation is non-covalent and depends on the activator staying bound, so washing away small molecules would let the modulator dissociate and the enzyme would revert to its inactive conformation. (Choice D) is incorrect: reversible inhibitor binding is governed by equilibrium, so removing or diluting the components would release the enzyme rather than lock in a persistent state. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that a regulatory change surviving removal of all stimuli and small molecules must be irreversible, which points to proteolytic activation of a zymogen rather than any reversible covalent or non-covalent mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = '06738ec8-0cfb-4685-be16-119f21663264';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is A because a regulatory DNA-binding protein achieves its effect by attaching to one particular base sequence, so destroying that sequence abolishes the attachment and eliminates the protein's control over transcription. (Choice A) is correct: the protein's binding function depends on the identity of the bases at that site, so scrambling the base order removes the site the protein attaches to, and without attachment the protein can no longer regulate the gene. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that a DNA-binding regulatory protein attaches to DNA non-specifically (the way histones coat DNA regardless of sequence), which would predict unchanged binding after the sequence is scrambled; regulatory proteins of this type instead depend on a particular base sequence. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the relationship, assuming that changing the target sequence would improve binding, whereas altering the bases the protein is matched to weakens or abolishes—not strengthens—the interaction. (Choice D) is incorrect: this confuses an upstream regulatory site with the coding region; the scrambled sequence lies in the promoter and is not translated, so the encoded amino acid sequence is unaffected even though regulation is disrupted. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a regulatory DNA-binding protein carries out its function by attaching to a specific base sequence, so that its ability to control a gene depends on that sequence being intact.$px$ WHERE id = '067eb928-d6e0-4a06-9bfe-a3b629f35cd5';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$An infant with a CPT-I deficiency develops profound hypoglycemia during an overnight fast, yet — unlike a child with a defect in gluconeogenesis — shows strikingly LOW blood ketone levels for the degree of hypoglycemia. Which consequence of the enzyme defect best accounts for this "hypoketotic" pattern during fasting?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is D because CPT-I is the entry point for long-chain fatty acids into the mitochondrial matrix. When it is defective, long-chain fat cannot be oxidized, so the liver produces little acetyl-CoA from fat during fasting. Ketone bodies are made FROM that acetyl-CoA, so ketones stay low (hypoketotic). At the same time, the fat that normally spares glucose during a fast cannot be burned, so glucose is consumed and hypoglycemia results.

(Choice D) is correct: it links the single lesion — no long-chain fatty acid entry — to both findings: low acetyl-CoA (hence low ketones) and loss of fat-derived fuel (hence hypoglycemia).
(Choice A) is incorrect: the liver lacks SCOT and cannot consume ketone bodies; it is a net exporter, so it does not oxidize them before export.
(Choice B) is incorrect: with CPT-I blocked, long-chain acyl-CoA does NOT accumulate in the matrix — it never gets in. There is no matrix surplus to divert into cholesterol.
(Choice C) is incorrect: CPT-I and HMG-CoA synthase are distinct enzymes; the shuttle defect starves ketogenesis of substrate rather than inactivating the ketogenic enzyme.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: you must trace a rate-limiting transport block forward to its downstream fasting consequences rather than recall a labeled syndrome.$px$ WHERE id = '077a582a-4095-491e-b87f-16281fcc3410';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is B because, once the synaptonemal complex breaks down and the cohesin linking the homologous pair is removed, the only remaining physical connection holding the two homologs of a tetrad together is the chiasma. The text states that after the synaptonemal complex is gone "the pairs are held together only at the chiasmata" and that "There must be at least one chiasma per chromosome for proper separation of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I." With no chiasma on chromosome 7, its homologs are untethered as the cell reaches metaphase/anaphase I, so the spindle cannot reliably pull one homolog to each pole; the text identifies failure of homologous pairs to separate—often from "misaligned or incomplete synapsis"—as nondisjunction. (Choice A) is wrong because sister chromatids are held at the centromere by cohesin "until anaphase II," not by chiasmata, so they normally separate in meiosis II regardless of crossover. (Choice B) is correct because the chiasma is the sole tether remaining after synaptonemal-complex and cohesin-arm dissolution, and its absence permits improper homolog segregation (nondisjunction). (Choice C) is wrong because DNA replication occurs in S phase before prophase I and is independent of chiasma formation, so the chromosome still enters the tetrad with four chromatids. (Choice D) is wrong because spindle microtubules attach at the kinetochore on the centromere, not at the chiasma, so loss of a chiasma does not eliminate the attachment site needed for metaphase alignment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting a downstream segregation outcome at anaphase I from a specific molecular lesion by chaining the role of the chiasma as the last homolog tether to the definition of nondisjunction.$px$ WHERE id = '07d115cb-c521-4cd9-8867-8fb1a8c4635b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Separation and Purification Methods (BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the electrophoretic force on a species is \(F = qE\), the product of its net charge \(q\) and the field strength \(E\). As aspartate migrates through the pH gradient, the local pH continuously changes its net charge: at a pH above its pI it is deprotonated and net negative (and moves toward the anode/lower pH), while at a pH below its pI it is protonated and net positive (and moves toward the cathode/higher pH). At the point where the gradient pH equals the pI of \(2.8\), the positive and negative charges on aspartate exactly cancel, giving \(q = 0\). With zero net charge, \(F = qE = 0\) regardless of the field strength, so the field can no longer drive the molecule, and it stops — it "focuses." The band is sharp and self-correcting: any drift toward higher pH makes the molecule net negative and pulls it back toward the anode, and any drift toward lower pH makes it net positive and pushes it back, so it returns to the \(\text{pH} = \text{pI}\) position. (Choice A) This imports the size-sieving mechanism of SDS-PAGE; isoelectric focusing separates by charge/pI within a pH gradient, and stopping is set by where net charge reaches zero, not by pore size or molecular size. (Choice B) This reverses the actual charge relationship: at its pI a molecule's net charge is at a minimum (zero), not a maximum; a larger charge would produce a larger force and faster migration, not anchoring. (Choice C) Although solubility is often lowest near the pI, isoelectric focusing stops the molecule because the driving force vanishes at zero net charge, not because of precipitation; the molecule remains in solution and would resume migrating if the local pH were changed. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '0819468e-a4c7-4751-895b-babc0377d1fc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because percent ionization is the fraction of the initial acid that has dissociated, expressed as a percentage: \(\text{percent ionization} = \dfrac{x}{C}\times 100\%\), where \(x = [\text{H}^+]\) produced at equilibrium and \(C\) is the initial acid concentration. Here \(x = 9.0\times10^{-5}\,\text{M}\) and \(C = 0.25\,\text{M}\), so \(\dfrac{9.0\times10^{-5}}{0.25} = 3.6\times10^{-4}\), and multiplying by \(100\%\) gives \(3.6\times10^{-4}\times100\% = 0.036\%\). The very small value confirms that \(\text{HClO}\) is a weak acid that ionizes only slightly. (Choice A) The value \(0.00036\%\) is \(\dfrac{9.0\times10^{-5}}{0.25} = 3.6\times10^{-4}\) reported directly as a percent, omitting the multiplication by \(100\%\) that converts the dimensionless fraction into a percentage. (Choice C) The value \(3.6\%\) comes from applying the factor of \(100\) twice—correctly forming \(3.6\times10^{-4}\) and then multiplying by \(10^4\) instead of \(10^2\)—an order-of-magnitude scaling error. (Choice D) The value \(0.0090\%\) comes from computing \([\text{H}^+]\times100\% = 9.0\times10^{-5}\times100\%\) without dividing by the initial concentration \(C\), so the ionized amount is never normalized to how much acid was present. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '08441ee7-d3d0-43c9-ab2a-f1ff5bdfff8e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Irreversible inhibitors such as iodoacetamide work by covalently modifying an essential side chain (e.g., a cysteine) in or near the active site, so activity is not restored by dialysis or dilution. A classic way to identify and protect that essential residue is to first occupy the active site with a saturating concentration of a reversibly binding ligand: the bound ligand physically blocks the reactive residue, so the covalent inhibitor cannot reach it, and once the protecting ligand is washed away the enzyme is active. Consistent with this, irreversible inhibitors permanently inactivate an enzyme by chemically modifying key amino acid side chains—for example, cysteine alkylation by iodoacetamide—and the essential residue can be shielded by first saturating the active site with a ligand that binds reversibly there, after which the critical side chain is protected from chemical modification. (Choice A) is correct: the results show the analog protected the enzyme only because iodoacetamide targets an essential active-site residue—reversible occupancy of that site blocked the covalent reaction, and the covalent nature of the inhibition is why dialysis alone did not rescue Sample 1. (Choice B) is incorrect: a substrate analog binds the active site, not a separate allosteric site, and if the inhibition were merely reversible binding at an allosteric site, dialysis would have restored Sample 1 as well—yet Sample 1 stayed inactive, which is the hallmark of covalent (irreversible) modification. (Choice C) is incorrect: no substrate is present during dialysis and the analog is washed away, so 'outcompeting for substrate' cannot explain the recovery; competition for substrate describes competitive reversible inhibition, not protection against a covalent reagent. (Choice D) is incorrect: iodoacetamide inactivates by covalently alkylating a specific residue, not by denaturing the fold, and a small alkylating reagent occupying one side chain would not be reversed by refolding after dialysis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of irreversible covalent inhibition to interpret a protection experiment and infer that the inhibitor targets an essential active-site residue.$px$ WHERE id = '08698680-01f7-4344-ab72-28cca2031476';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is B because every component of a blood vessel — the inner endothelial lining as well as the surrounding smooth muscle and connective tissue — traces back to the embryonic mesoderm. During early development, blood vessels form from the embryonic mesoderm: precursor cells called hemangioblasts differentiate into angioblasts, which give rise to the vessels, and the endothelial lining arises from these mesoderm-derived angioblasts while surrounding mesenchymal cells give rise to the smooth muscle and connective tissue layers. The endothelial lining is a tempting trap because its name evokes "endoderm," but the epithelium that lines vessels of the lymphatic and cardiovascular system in fact derives from the mesoderm and is called an endothelium. The endoderm gives rise to the lining of the airways and digestive tract, not to the lining of hollow organs that do not connect to the exterior such as blood vessels, which are instead lined by mesoderm-derived endothelium. (Choice A) is incorrect because it assigns the endothelium to the endoderm, but the vessel endothelium derives from the mesoderm; the endoderm lines the digestive and respiratory tracts, not vessels. (Choice B) is correct because the endothelium arises from mesodermal angioblasts and the wall's smooth muscle and connective tissue arise from surrounding mesenchyme, so all three are mesodermal. (Choice C) is incorrect because the ectoderm gives rise to surface epithelia such as the skin and the lining of the mouth and anus, not the endothelium, which is mesodermal. (Choice D) is incorrect because, although blood vessels are hollow internal tubes, those that do not open to the exterior are lined by mesoderm-derived endothelium rather than by endoderm, which lines the gut. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because the student must reconcile the misleading "endo-" terminology with the established germ-layer assignments and chain the endothelium-versus-wall distinction to the single conclusion that the entire vessel is mesodermal.$px$ WHERE id = '08819010-35e8-4793-81cd-df918c46cd5c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is D because the demand signal (rising \(\text{ADP}\)) and the supply pathway are linked through isocitrate dehydrogenase: \(\text{ADP}\) is a positive allosteric effector of that enzyme, so raising \(\text{ADP}\) accelerates the TCA cycle, which produces more \(\text{NADH}\) and \(\text{FADH}_2\). Those extra reduced carriers feed the electron transport chain, so proton pumping and O\(_2\) reduction speed up at the same time that \(\text{ATP}\) synthase — now well-supplied with \(\text{ADP}\) — runs faster. Supply and demand therefore rise in concert.

(Choice D) is correct: \(\text{ADP}\) recruits supply by activating a supply-limiting TCA enzyme, so more \(\text{NADH}\) reaches the chain exactly when more \(\text{ATP}\) is being made.

(Choice A) is incorrect: while \(\text{ADP}\) is indeed the synthase's substrate, this choice ignores the stem's point — that faster synthase demands more \(\text{NADH}\) supply; the chain cannot sustain higher flux without the reducing equivalents that \(\text{ADP}\)-activated isocitrate dehydrogenase provides.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the regulation — \(\text{ADP}\) activates, not inhibits, isocitrate dehydrogenase; inhibiting it would cut \(\text{NADH}\) supply and slow, not speed, oxphos.

(Choice C) is incorrect: enlarging the proton gradient does not create \(\text{NADH}\); in fact a larger gradient opposes further pumping and would slow the chain, so it cannot be the mechanism that raises \(\text{NADH}\) supply.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason about how a demand signal (\(\text{ADP}\)) recruits matching substrate supply through allosteric regulation.$px$ WHERE id = '09866052-7803-48a7-958f-126c264f53d8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The International Union of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology (IUBMB) sorts enzymes into six EC classes by reaction type: oxidoreductases (EC1) run redox reactions, transferases (EC2) catalyze "transfer/exchange of group from one molecule to another," hydrolases (EC3) run hydrolysis reactions, lyases (EC4) perform "elimination forming a double bond," isomerases (EC5) catalyze "conversions of geometric, stereo- or constitutive isomers," and ligases (EC6) drive "condensation of two molecules into one." The decisive question here is not whether a phosphate ends up in a new place, but whether a group is handed to a second molecule or simply relocated within the same one. (Choice A) is correct: glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate are constitutional (positional) isomers of one another, and the reaction repositions the phosphate on a single molecule with no atoms gained or lost and no partner molecule, which is exactly the isomerase (EC5) definition of interconverting constitutive isomers. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a phosphate does move, a transferase (EC2) transfers a group "from one molecule to another"—it requires a separate acceptor or donor molecule (as when a kinase moves phosphate from \(\text{ATP}\) onto a substrate); here only one molecule enters and only one leaves, so no intermolecular transfer occurs. (Choice C) is incorrect: a hydrolase (EC3) uses water to cleave a bond, but the reaction adds no water and leaves the phosphate esterified rather than hydrolyzed off. (Choice D) is incorrect: a ligase (EC6) joins two separate molecules into one, usually powered by \(\text{ATP}\); no second molecule is joined and no cofactor is consumed in this intramolecular rearrangement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the EC classification scheme to a described reaction and distinguish an intramolecular rearrangement (isomerase) from an intermolecular group transfer (transferase) rather than reacting to the fact that a phosphate changed position.$px$ WHERE id = '09da5d3c-49e2-4e10-929e-8ad7f8d6f21f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.' Information flows through a neuron in one direction—from the dendrites, across the cell body, and down the axon—giving the neuron its polarity; the action potential is generated at the trigger zone (axon hillock plus initial segment) and then propagates along the axon to the axon terminals and synaptic end bulbs, where it triggers neurotransmitter release onto the target. The scenario fixes the problem downstream of impulse generation but upstream of the target: the action potential is made normally, yet it never arrives to excite the next cell, and both the release machinery and the target receptors are known to be intact. (Choice A) is incorrect: the dendrites lie on the input side of the neuron, receiving synaptic signals and carrying them inward to the cell body; blocking them would prevent input, but here the action potential is already being generated normally. (Choice B) is incorrect: the cell body sits before the trigger zone in the signal path, so a lesion there would impair generation of the impulse, yet the stem states the action potential is produced normally at the trigger zone. (Choice C) is correct: the axon is the conducting output fiber that carries the action potential away from the trigger zone toward the terminals, so a drug that blocks propagation along the axon would stop the already-generated impulse from ever reaching the target, exactly matching the observation. (Choice D) is incorrect: the postsynaptic membrane belongs to the target cell and carries its receptors, which the stem specifies are intact, so the failure lies in the presynaptic neuron's own output pathway rather than at reception. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fixed directional flow of signals through a neuron to locate where an intact impulse could be halted before reaching its target.$px$ WHERE id = '0a2c3b3d-8bfb-4bf8-8320-df7270c98473';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is A because the viable count quadruples over each 2-hour interval from hour 4 onward (\(4.0\times10^{4}\) → \(1.6\times10^{5}\) → \(6.4\times10^{5}\)), an exponential climb that identifies the culture as currently in the log (exponential) phase, when cells are dividing fastest and a drug that kills only dividing cells does the most damage. In the log phase "the cells are actively dividing by binary fission and their number increases exponentially," and the text notes the log phase "is also the stage where bacteria are the most susceptible to the action of disinfectants and common antibiotics that affect protein, DNA, and cell-wall synthesis." A beta-lactam blocks cell-wall (peptidoglycan) synthesis, so it can only harm a cell that is actively building new wall to divide; the maximum proportion of such cells exists during exponential growth, which the latest timepoints show is happening now. Waiting changes the phase to one with fewer dividing cells, reducing the drug's effect. (Choice A) Correct: the steady exponential rise at the final timepoints marks an actively dividing log-phase culture, the phase of greatest susceptibility to a cell-wall-synthesis inhibitor. (Choice B) Tempting partial truth: a stationary-phase plateau does hold many live cells, but in stationary phase "the synthesis of peptidoglycans, proteins, and nucleic-acids" slows, so "stationary cultures are less susceptible to antibiotics that disrupt these processes" — cells that are not building wall are not killed by a beta-lactam, so total cell number is irrelevant. (Choice C) The flat count from hour 0 to 2 is the lag phase, when "the number of cells does not change" because cells are gearing up but not yet dividing; non-dividing cells are not building new wall, so a beta-lactam is least, not most, effective then. (Choice D) Reversed/false: the drug's lethality is phase-dependent precisely because it requires active cell-wall synthesis; phases differ greatly in the fraction of dividing cells, so the kill rate is not uniform across phases. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must read the viable-count-versus-time values, recognize the exponential (fixed-fold-per-interval) pattern as the log phase, and link that data-derived phase to the drug's mechanism to predict the optimal timing.$px$ WHERE id = '0a90a345-ff90-4e7e-98f6-1fe702382558';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The behavior described is the induced-fit model of enzyme-substrate binding: contact with the correct substrate changes the enzyme's shape so the active site molds around that substrate and the reaction can proceed. In the induced-fit model, contact between the reactant and the protein alters the protein's conformation so that it can bind, and this substrate-driven change in the enzyme's shape lets the active site fit the substrate more closely and mediate the reaction, unlike the lock-and-key model in which the substrate binds without any further change to the enzyme's shape. The key insight is that hexokinase's catalytic machinery is assembled only by the conformational change, and only the genuine substrate can trigger that change. (Choice A) is correct: because the closed, catalytically competent geometry is produced only when the correct substrate itself drives the lobes shut, a molecule that cannot induce closure is never positioned for phosphoryl transfer, so making catalysis contingent on substrate-triggered closure is what confers the observed selectivity. (Choice B) is incorrect: Mg2+ genuinely coordinates and neutralizes ATP's negatively charged phosphates, but that cofactor chemistry does not depend on the lobe-closing motion and does not explain why non-substrate molecules are ignored; the metal requirement would apply whether the enzyme were rigid or flexible. (Choice C) is incorrect: it overgeneralizes transition-state stabilization by claiming the closed cleft speeds the reaction for "any molecule they happen to enclose" — that would predict wasteful reaction with whatever is bound and cannot account for selectivity, since specificity here comes from which molecule can trigger closure, not from indiscriminate rate enhancement once closed. (Choice D) is incorrect: while it is true that solvent is excluded from the closed cleft, its claim that the enzyme's shape "stays fixed and identical whether or not glucose is present" directly contradicts the X-ray observation that the lobes swing shut around glucose; it describes a rigid lock-and-key enzyme and denies the conformational change the scenario reports. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the induced-fit model to evaluate a proposed mechanistic explanation and identify the principle that actually produces the enzyme's selectivity.$px$ WHERE id = '0b0f8f8b-e512-4206-8e0f-0827821b5291';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is C because the somatic motor pathway to skeletal muscle uses a single lower motor neuron that projects from the spinal cord directly to the muscle, whereas the autonomic pathway to the heart inserts a synapse in a ganglion, splitting the route into two neurons. In a somatic reflex, the output is the lower motor neuron in the ventral horn of the spinal cord, which projects directly to a skeletal muscle; in a visceral reflex, the output is a two-step chain in which a preganglionic fiber runs to a ganglion and a postganglionic fiber then projects to the target effector. Because the autonomic command to the SA node must cross the preganglionic-to-postganglionic synapse in a ganglion, it is interrupted; only the somatic command to the biceps is carried uninterrupted by one neuron from cord to effector. (Choice A) is wrong because the autonomic preganglionic neuron does not travel uninterrupted to the organ—it synapses on a ganglionic (postganglionic) neuron, so the heart is reached by two neurons in series. (Choice B) is wrong because it reverses the autonomic rule by treating every peripheral motor pathway as one-neuron; the autonomic efferent branch is explicitly a two-neuron chain. (Choice C) is correct because the somatic lower motor neuron projects from the ventral horn directly to skeletal muscle with no intervening synapse, satisfying the single-uninterrupted-neuron condition. (Choice D) is wrong because it misapplies the autonomic ganglionic relay to somatic output; the somatic system provides output strictly to skeletal muscles via a single motor neuron and does not relay through a ganglion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the structural distinction between somatic and autonomic efferent wiring to a specific scenario to predict which command travels via one uninterrupted neuron.$px$ WHERE id = '0b125d29-6435-4e06-a5b1-3ac834d4797a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is C because raising genetic variability (here, by out-crossing to unrelated foxes) works through two effects: it makes carrier-by-carrier matings rarer, and—when such a mating does occur—the basic recessive cross still affects only one quarter of the offspring. Increasing a population's genetic variability reduces the inbreeding-associated risk of bringing deleterious recessive mutations together: the chance that two carriers will mate is low, and even then, only 25 percent of their offspring inherit the disease allele from both parents; a monohybrid \(Aa \times Aa\) cross indeed yields \(\tfrac{1}{4}\) (\(25\%\)) affected \(aa\) offspring. So statements I and III are both correct. Statement II is false: the allele maintains itself at low levels in the gene pool precisely because selection cannot swiftly eliminate it—variability dilutes the risk of expression, it does not purge the allele. (Choice A) is wrong because it includes the true statement I but discards the equally correct statement III about the \(25\%\) recessive ratio. (Choice B) is wrong because it includes the true statement III but discards statement I, the central mechanism by which variability lowers the chance two carriers meet. (Choice C) is correct: statements I and III are both directly supported, while the false statement II is excluded. (Choice D) is wrong because it pairs true statement III with false statement II, which overstates outbreeding as eliminating the allele rather than reducing the frequency of its expression. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how increasing genetic variability reduces the expression of deleterious recessive alleles without removing them from the gene pool.$px$ WHERE id = '0b1dabf4-de53-4a51-bf82-6db249929100';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because mitochondria carry their own DNA and are delivered to the zygote almost entirely through the egg cytoplasm, so only mothers transmit a mitochondrially encoded trait, and they transmit it to all offspring regardless of sex. Mitochondria have their own ribosomes and DNA, genetic material physically separate from the nuclear DNA housed in the nucleus. Because the sperm contributes essentially no cytoplasm to the zygote, the offspring's mitochondria (and the alleles they carry) come from the egg; an affected mother therefore passes the trait to every child, while an affected father passes it to none. This maternal, cytoplasmic transmission is non-Mendelian precisely because the responsible gene lies outside the nuclear chromosomes that segregate during meiosis. (Choice A) Autosomal dominant inheritance involves a nuclear chromosome gene transmitted equally through eggs and sperm, so an affected father would pass it to about half his children—contradicting the observation that he transmits to none. (Choice B) X-linked dominant inheritance is also nuclear and would let an affected father transmit the trait to all of his daughters (who receive his X), which is not seen here. (Choice C) Autosomal recessive inheritance is nuclear and would not produce transmission exclusively through the mother; both parents contribute the gene, and the recessive phenotype typically skips generations rather than passing from one affected parent to every child. (Choice D) Maternal cytoplasmic inheritance correctly accounts for transmission only from the mother to all offspring, because the relevant gene resides on mitochondrial DNA delivered via the egg cytoplasm; the nuance is that the pattern is sex-independent in offspring but strictly sex-dependent in the transmitting parent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that mitochondria carry their own DNA inherited through the egg cytoplasm to predict a non-Mendelian pedigree pattern.$px$ WHERE id = '0b2d44ee-e48e-4b18-987b-67af0d2ab2d3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Bone's rigid extracellular matrix is a composite of an organic component (mostly collagen fibers) embedded in a mineralized ground substance containing hydroxyapatite, a form of calcium phosphate, and each component contributes a distinct mechanical property: the text states that "without collagen, bones would be brittle and shatter easily" and that "without mineral crystals, bones would flex and provide little support" (lines 7015-7016). Selectively dissolving the mineral therefore removes the source of hardness and rigidity while leaving the flexible collagen scaffold behind. (Choice A) is correct: with the hydroxyapatite gone, only the collagen framework remains, so the demineralized bone flexes and can no longer provide rigid support, exactly the outcome the text predicts when mineral crystals are absent. (Choice B) is incorrect: brittleness and shattering are what result from losing the collagen ("without collagen, bones would be brittle and shatter easily"), so this reverses which component was removed; the intact collagen actually makes the treated bone tough and pliable, not brittle. (Choice C) is incorrect: the acid dissolves the inorganic hydroxyapatite, not the collagen, so the organic scaffold persists and preserves the bone's overall shape rather than collapsing into a shapeless mass. (Choice D) is incorrect: collagen supplies flexibility and tensile strength, not stiffness, so removing the mineral removes the rigidity and the bone cannot retain its full stiffness. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the division of labor within the calcium-protein matrix—mineral providing rigidity, collagen providing flexibility—to predict the mechanical result of removing one component.$px$ WHERE id = '0b359fce-abbe-478d-8daa-518f6c86989b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is A because the \(\text{pK}_a\) of an ionizable group is, by definition, the \(\text{pH}\) at which that group is exactly half deprotonated. When \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_a\), the functional group is 50% deprotonated, because the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation reduces to \(0 = \log([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}])\), giving \([\text{A}^-] = [\text{HA}]\) (a 50/50 mixture). Since the thiol's \(\text{pK}_a\) is \(8.3\), the half-deprotonated point is exactly \(\text{pH} = 8.3\). (Choice A) is correct: setting \(\text{pH}\) equal to the \(\text{pK}_a\) of \(8.3\) makes \([-\text{S}^-] = [-\text{SH}]\), the 50% deprotonated condition. (Choice B) is wrong because \(2\) units below the \(\text{pK}_a\) the group is about 99% protonated, not half deprotonated — that is the "fully protonated" extreme, not the midpoint. (Choice C) is wrong because \(2\) units above the \(\text{pK}_a\) the group is about 99% deprotonated, the opposite extreme from half. (Choice D) is wrong because \(\text{pH} = 7.0\) is below the \(\text{pK}_a\) of \(8.3\), so the thiol is mostly protonated; \(7.0\) is the neutral point of pure water, which is unrelated to this group's \(\text{pK}_a\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between \(\text{pH}\) and \(\text{pK}_a\) to identify the half-deprotonation point for a specific side chain.$px$ WHERE id = '0b5d66d2-0840-462a-a644-0ae888aaf77d';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$Bicarbonate \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) is amphiprotic and can function on either side of a Brønsted–Lowry reaction. In one such reaction, a bicarbonate ion accepts a proton from a neighboring species. Which species is the conjugate acid of bicarbonate that results from this reaction?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).' In Brønsted–Lowry terms, an amphiprotic species such as bicarbonate can either donate or accept a proton; when it accepts a proton it acts as a base, and a base that accepts \(\text{H}^+\) is thereby converted into its conjugate acid. The key inference is that this reaction runs in the protonation direction (charge becomes one unit less negative), not the deprotonation direction that bicarbonate more familiarly undergoes. (Choice A) is correct: accepting one proton adds \(\text{H}^+\) to \(\text{HCO}_3^-\), raising the charge from \(-1\) to \(0\) and giving carbonic acid, \(\text{HCO}_3^- + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), which is the conjugate acid of bicarbonate. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) is the conjugate base of bicarbonate, formed when bicarbonate instead donates a proton; this is the reverse of the process described, and the stem specifies that bicarbonate accepts a proton. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) is the conjugate acid of water, produced when water accepts a proton; it is a plausible product elsewhere in the system but is not derived from bicarbonate, so it cannot be bicarbonate's conjugate acid. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{OH}^-\) would form if bicarbonate acted on water, but a conjugate acid must be the original species with one added proton, so the correct product must retain the carbon and all three oxygens of bicarbonate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of a conjugate acid to bicarbonate acting in its less-familiar basic role and determine the correct protonation product rather than reflexively deprotonating it.$px$ WHERE id = '0b774690-dd8e-4576-9070-fb79f1667309';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because a sustained surplus—taking in more energy than is expended over a significant period—means the unused energy is deposited, chiefly as triacylglycerol in adipose tissue, so fat stores accumulate and body mass rises.

(Choice B) is correct: when intake exceeds expenditure week after week, the extra energy is stored as fat, so adipose stores grow and body mass climbs.
(Choice A) is incorrect: mobilizing stored triacylglycerol and losing mass is the consequence of a deficit (intake below expenditure), which is the opposite of the described surplus.
(Choice C) is incorrect: although basal metabolic rate does rise somewhat as body mass increases, expenditure does not instantly and exactly cancel a chronic surplus, so weight does not stay perfectly constant.
(Choice D) is incorrect: preferential breakdown of lean muscle occurs during an energy deficit or starvation, not during a surplus in which energy is being stored as fat.

This item tests Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving (Skill 2): you must reason from the described intake-versus-expenditure situation to its body-mass consequence rather than recall a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '0b83060c-d8e3-435c-a4a2-23c4c6ff0cff';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because every electrode identity, the wire electron direction, and the sign of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) must each be tracked separately for the two modes, and all three flip together when the cell is forced backward. In the spontaneous galvanic cell the species with the higher standard reduction potential is reduced: \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) (\(+0.80\ \text{V}\)) is reduced at the cathode and copper metal is oxidized at the anode, giving the spontaneous reaction \(\text{Cu} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+} \rightarrow \text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag}\). Copper is therefore the anode (the negative terminal of a galvanic cell), and electrons always travel from the anode to the cathode through the external wire, i.e. copper-to-silver. The cell potential is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 0.80 - 0.34 = +0.46\ \text{V}\), which is positive, consistent with a spontaneous galvanic process (\(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)). When an external supply forces the identical chemistry to run in reverse, \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag} \rightarrow \text{Cu} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+}\), the half-reaction roles swap: copper ion is now reduced, so the copper electrode becomes the cathode, and silver is now oxidized at the anode. Because electrons still exit the anode and enter the cathode, the wire direction reverses to silver-to-copper. The driven direction is the non-spontaneous one, so its standard cell potential is the negative of the galvanic value: \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 0.34 - 0.80 = -0.46\ \text{V}\), the hallmark of an electrolytic cell (\(\Delta G^{\circ} > 0\), requiring external work). All three features—copper switching anode→cathode, electron flow reversing copper-to-silver→silver-to-copper, and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) going \(+0.46\ \text{V} \rightarrow -0.46\ \text{V}\)—match only choice C. (Choice A) This reverses which metal is oxidized in the galvanic cell, naming silver as the galvanic anode; that error stems from treating the higher reduction potential of \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) as a tendency to be oxidized, when in fact the higher \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) means \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) is preferentially reduced and copper is the species oxidized at the anode. (Choice B) This holds copper fixed as the anode in both modes, so it keeps the electron flow as copper-to-silver in both; it correctly negates \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) but fails to recognize that forcing the reverse reaction swaps every electrode's role, making copper the cathode and reversing the wire direction. (Choice D) This correctly swaps copper to the cathode and reverses the electron flow but keeps \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) positive in both modes; it overlooks that driving the non-spontaneous reverse direction inverts the sign of the cell potential to \(-0.46\ \text{V}\), even though the chemistry involves the same couple. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '0ba8d4ec-a834-4eb7-aa2b-7450ffcbb42c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is A because the egg contributes the entire cytoplasmic compartment of the zygote, so every mitochondrion (and its DNA) in the embryo is egg-derived, making mtDNA disorders maternally inherited. The text states that "sperm contribute only DNA at fertilization—not cytoplasm. Therefore, the cytoplasm and all of the cytoplasmic organelles in the developing embryo are of egg-derived origin. This includes mitochondria, which contain their own DNA," and that mtDNA "was maternally inherited." Because the sperm delivers essentially only its nuclear chromosomes, any paternal mitochondria are not propagated, so a mtDNA mutation present in the mother's eggs is the only mitochondrial genome the child can receive. (Choice A) is correct: the ovum's large cytoplasm carries the mitochondria that populate the zygote, so the disease tracks the maternal line. (Choice B) is wrong because paternal mitochondria do not persist—mtDNA degrades in the sperm or the sperm's flagellar mitochondria fail to enter the egg—so there is no equal paternal contribution. (Choice C) is wrong because, unlike biparental nuclear DNA, mtDNA is inherited uniparentally from the mother rather than recombining and being passed half by each parent. (Choice D) is wrong because the zona pellucida mediates sperm binding and the acrosomal reaction, not the sorting of which mitochondria enter the zygote. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting an inheritance pattern in a clinical scenario and tracing it back to the cell-biological mechanism of differential gamete cytoplasmic contribution.$px$ WHERE id = '0baaa4f8-fd78-48cb-8435-62a897414afb';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Cofactors are molecules that bind to enzymes and are required for catalytic activity; they divide into metals and coenzymes, where coenzymes are small organic molecules that are often derived from vitamins. Crucially, coenzymes can bind loosely with the enzyme and be released from the active site, so they are also considered substrates for the reaction — the dissociable, freely-diffusing electron carrier described in the stem (the classic role of a vitamin-derived coenzyme such as NAD+, which acts as a dehydrogenase's substrate). Because this molecule leaves the enzyme entirely and is re-oxidized elsewhere in solution before it can act again, it is behaving as a loose, dissociable coenzyme, not a tightly bound cofactor. (Choice A) is correct: a coenzyme is a small, often vitamin-derived organic cofactor, and one that binds loosely, leaves the active site carrying the electrons, and is regenerated as a free species before returning is behaving as a dissociable coenzyme (co-substrate), matching every detail of the stem. (Choice B) is incorrect: tight-binding coenzymes are referred to as prosthetic groups, and such cofactors cannot dissociate easily from the enzyme — a prosthetic group stays bound and is regenerated in place, which directly contradicts a molecule that leaves the active site and diffuses away each turnover, so the loose, dissociable behavior described rules this term out. (Choice C) is incorrect: an apoenzyme is the protein enzyme not yet associated with a required cofactor, i.e., the polypeptide lacking its cofactor — it is the enzyme itself in an inactive state, not the small organic molecule that binds to it. (Choice D) is incorrect: a holoenzyme is the assembled complex of the enzyme bound with its required cofactors, so it names the whole catalytically active enzyme-plus-cofactor unit, not the individual small molecule that transiently binds and departs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the loose- versus tight-binding criterion for cofactors to classify a dissociable, regenerable organic molecule as a coenzyme rather than a prosthetic group, apoenzyme, or holoenzyme.$px$ WHERE id = '0bc0774b-d17f-4ab2-a0c1-677deaae1279';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because identifying the oxidizing agent here requires recognizing that in nitric-acid chemistry the oxidant is the nitrogen of the nitrate, not the acidic proton, and then counting electrons from the oxidation-state changes through the stoichiometry of the balanced equation. First, locate the species whose oxidation state falls. Zinc rises from \(0\) to \(+2\), so Zn is oxidized and is the reducing agent. The protons are not reduced—no \(\text{H}_2\) is evolved (the stem states this explicitly), so \(\text{H}^+\) acts only as the acid, not as the oxidant. Of the eight nitrogen atoms entering as \(\text{HNO}_3\) (all at \(+5\)), six emerge unchanged at \(+5\) in the three \(\text{Zn(NO}_3)_2\) units (which contain \(3 \times 2 = 6\) nitrate ions); only the two nitrogen atoms that become \(\text{NO}\) are reduced. In \(\text{NO}\), oxygen is \(-2\) and the molecule is neutral, so that nitrogen is \(+2\). Each reduced nitrogen therefore changes \(+5 \rightarrow +2\), a gain of \(3\) electrons, and with two such nitrogen atoms the reduction accepts \(2 \times 3 = 6\) electrons. Cross-check with the oxidation side: three Zn each lose \(2\) electrons for \(3 \times 2 = 6\) electrons released, exactly matching the \(6\) electrons accepted. The oxidizing agent is \(\text{HNO}_3\) (acting through its nitrate nitrogen), and \(6\) mol of electrons are transferred per mole of equation. (Choice A) names \(\text{H}^+\) as the oxidizing agent and assumes proton reduction to \(\text{H}_2\); however, with dilute nitric acid no hydrogen gas is produced, so the protons are not reduced, and \(2\) electrons would be the count only for a hypothetical \(\text{H}_2\)-evolving reaction that does not occur here. (Choice B) correctly identifies \(\text{HNO}_3\) as the oxidant but uses the wrong product depth: a \(+5 \rightarrow -3\) change corresponds to reduction all the way to the ammonium ion \(\text{NH}_4^+\), which forms only with very dilute acid, not to the \(\text{NO}\) (\(+2\)) specified by this equation, so it gives \(8\) electrons per reduced nitrogen instead of the \(3\) that apply here. (Choice C) reverses the agent roles: zinc's oxidation state increases, so zinc is oxidized and is the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent, even though the \(6\)-electron total it cites happens to equal the electrons zinc releases. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a balance check of the equation, the identification of the true oxidant in nitric-acid chemistry, the oxidation-state assignment of nitrogen in nitric oxide, and the electron stoichiometry to arrive at a single self-consistent answer.$px$ WHERE id = '0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).' Female meiosis is spread across a lifetime and paused at two checkpoints: a primary oocyte sits arrested in meiosis I from before birth, and only a pre-ovulatory luteinizing hormone surge triggers it to resume and complete meiosis I, yielding a secondary oocyte plus the first polar body. This secondary oocyte is what leaves the ovary during ovulation, and it remains arrested in metaphase of meiosis II until fertilization, so the ovulated cell is caught between the two divisions until fertilization occurs. (Choice A) is correct: the released cell has already completed meiosis I (that is why the LH surge preceded ovulation) and is arrested midway through meiosis II at metaphase II; meiosis II is completed only upon fertilization, and with no fertilization here, the cell stays in this second-division arrest and is cleared. (Choice B) is incorrect: although primary oocytes are indeed arrested in meiosis I, that arrest is broken by the LH surge before ovulation, so the cell in the oviduct has already advanced past meiosis I and is no longer a primary oocyte. (Choice C) is incorrect: the ovary does not release a finished gamete, because the single haploid ovum is produced only if a sperm cell penetrates the secondary oocyte, and no sperm is present. (Choice D) is incorrect: meiosis in this lineage began during fetal life, not at ovulation, so the ovulated cell is a haploid product of meiosis I, not a diploid precursor awaiting its first division. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the two-checkpoint timing of female meiosis to a specific physiological moment—an ovulated, unfertilized cell—rather than recall a gamete count.$px$ WHERE id = '0bfa26e0-39d7-44a1-9680-63f23a99fc3c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because degradation proceeds from chain termini one residue at a time, so the rate of glucose release scales with the number of termini that enzymes can attack at once. Glycogen's frequent α-1,6 branch points generate a highly branched, tree-like architecture in which every branch ends in its own terminus. An equal mass of unbranched amylose is essentially a single long chain with only one such terminus per molecule, so many enzyme molecules can chew glycogen from many ends in parallel, producing a large amount of free glucose almost simultaneously.

(Choice D) is correct: extensive branching multiplies the number of accessible chain ends, and because each end is an independent site of exolytic cleavage, more ends means proportionally faster overall glucose output — exactly what a rapid, on-demand energy source requires.

(Choice B) is incorrect: both glycogen and amylose are joined by the same α-1,4 glycosidic linkage along their main chains, so the intrinsic bond chemistry is identical. The difference in mobilization rate arises from architecture (number of ends), not from any difference in bond strength.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly senses that "more ends = faster release" but names the wrong end. Each linear glucose chain has exactly one reducing end (the terminus with a free anomeric carbon); branching adds non-reducing ends, and it is the non-reducing termini — those bearing a free C4 hydroxyl — from which glucose is removed. Branching does not multiply reducing ends.

(Choice A) is incorrect: branch points do make the molecule more compact, but exolytic enzymes act only at termini and do not "travel" processively down a chain to reach an interior target, so compactness per se does not speed release. The benefit of branching is the added termini, not a shorter path along any single chain.

Answering this requires reasoning from the described degradation mechanism — one residue removed at a time from free-C4-hydroxyl termini — to conclude that a structure maximizing the count of such termini best serves rapid, simultaneous glucose mobilization.$px$ WHERE id = '0c2659b2-973f-4459-8aed-223428c546c8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because the protein coat that surrounds and directly encloses a virus's nucleic acid genome is, by definition, the capsid. The text states that viruses "consist of a nucleic acid (either RNA or DNA, but never both) surrounded by a protein coat called a capsid," and that "each capsid is composed of protein subunits called capsomeres made of one or more different types of capsomere proteins that interlock to form the closely packed capsid." The micrograph shows exactly this arrangement: one nucleic acid molecule inside a geometric protein shell assembled from repeating subunits, with no lipid, which is the naked (nonenveloped) capsid. (Choice A) The envelope is incorrect because it is "a small portion of phospholipid membrane obtained as the virion buds from a host cell," a lipid bilayer outside the capsid — and the micrograph explicitly shows no lipid. (Choice B) A spike is incorrect because spikes are "protein structures" that extend "outward and away from the capsid" to "attach and enter a cell"; they are surface projections, not the shell enclosing the genome. (Choice C) A capsomere is incorrect because it names the individual "protein subunits" that "interlock to form the closely packed capsid" — a building block of the shell, not the whole shell that encloses the genome. (Choice D) The capsid is correct because it is the protein coat that surrounds the viral nucleic acid. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the protein shell enclosing a viral genome is called the capsid.$px$ WHERE id = '0c5cae55-a7a8-44d4-8bca-6cb9f9ea2a1d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development.' Fertilization must combine exactly one haploid sperm genome with one haploid egg genome to produce a single diploid zygote, so the egg has mechanisms to block additional sperm once the first has fused. The text describes a fast response in which "the membrane potential of the egg cell altered quickly" followed by a slow structural change, and warns that if "these mechanisms fail, multiple sperm can fuse with the egg, resulting in polyspermy" — a condition in which "the resulting embryo is not genetically viable and dies within a few days." (Choice A) is correct: removing the rapid membrane-potential change disables the fast block to polyspermy, so several sperm fuse and each contributes a genome, giving the embryo extra chromosome sets rather than a single diploid set, which is lethal. (Choice B) is incorrect: polyspermy is not harmless — each fused sperm delivers its nucleus, so the embryo does not simply use only the first sperm's genome and develop normally. (Choice C) is incorrect: it is the egg's own fast and slow blocks, not the acrosomal reaction, that prevent additional sperm from fusing; the acrosomal enzymes help a sperm penetrate the coat rather than seal it against other sperm. (Choice D) is incorrect: the surplus genomes are not selectively degraded to leave a haploid embryo, and a viable embryo requires the diploid condition, not a haploid one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the function of the block to polyspermy and the requirement for a single diploid genome to predict the outcome when the fast block is disabled.$px$ WHERE id = '0cf406c3-312f-413d-9914-f1608df6787b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' The electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) is only a rough guide to bond type and has many exceptions, so the most reliable predictor of ionic versus covalent character is the identity of the bonded atoms: bonds between two nonmetals are generally covalent (electrons shared, giving partial charges), whereas a bond between a metal and a nonmetal is often ionic (electron density essentially transferred, giving full ion charges). (Choice B) is correct: sodium is a Group 1 metal and iodine is a nonmetal, so \(\text{Na-I}\) is a metal-nonmetal bond and NaI is an ionic salt; the sodium atom donates its valence electron to iodine, producing \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{I}^-\) rather than a shared pair, which is what "predominantly ionic" means, even though its \(\Delta\text{EN}\) is not the largest listed. (Choice A) is incorrect: although \(\text{H-F}\) has the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) (\(\approx 1.9\)), both H and F are nonmetals, so the bond is polar covalent, not ionic—HF is a classic exception showing that a large \(\Delta\text{EN}\) alone does not guarantee an ionic bond. (Choice C) is incorrect: oxygen is indeed much more electronegative than carbon, but both are nonmetals, so \(\text{C-O}\) is a polar covalent bond with partial charges, not a transferred electron pair. (Choice D) is incorrect: N and H are both nonmetals and a small \(\Delta\text{EN}\) produces less charge separation, not more; \(\text{N-H}\) is polar covalent, and its modest partial charges are the opposite of ionic transfer. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that atom type and periodic-table position—not the electronegativity-difference value alone—best predict whether a bond is ionic or covalent.$px$ WHERE id = '0d02a150-7eaa-4921-b83d-1d5333ed5a0d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because ring opening to the free aldehyde requires a hemiacetal, and a glucose residue only retains a hemiacetal at its anomeric carbon (\(C1\)) if that carbon is NOT tied up in a glycosidic linkage. In an α-1,4 chain, each internal residue donates its \(C1\) into the acetal bond that connects it to the next residue, converting that anomeric carbon into a full acetal that cannot revert to the open-chain aldehyde. Following the chain, every residue uses its \(C1\) for a linkage except the single residue at one terminus, whose \(C1\) remains a free hemiacetal. That lone residue is the reducing end, and it is the only one that can open to an aldehyde and reduce Benedict's reagent.

(Choice A) is incorrect: wrong because not every residue is joined through its \(C1\). The residue at the reducing terminus keeps its \(C1\) free as a hemiacetal—it connects to the chain through its \(C4\) hydroxyl rather than through \(C1\)—so at least that one residue is not linked through its anomeric carbon and can still open to the aldehyde.

(Choice B) is incorrect: wrong because the two ends of the chain are not chemically equivalent. Only the reducing terminus has a free anomeric carbon. At the non-reducing terminus the residue's own \(C1\) is committed to the acetal glycosidic bond with its neighbor (only its \(C4\) hydroxyl is left free), so that residue cannot open to an aldehyde. This leaves a single reducing end per chain, not two.

(Choice D) is correct: because exactly one terminal residue keeps an uncommitted anomeric carbon, giving a single reducing end per unbranched chain.

(Choice C) is incorrect: wrong because a glucose whose \(C1\) is locked in an acetal glycosidic bond has lost its hemiacetal and can no longer open to the aldehyde; ring-opening ability is not preserved once the anomeric carbon is used in a linkage.

This is a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) question: it asks you to recall that a hemiacetal (free anomeric carbon) is required for ring opening and to apply that fact to trace which residues in a defined linear polysaccharide retain one.$px$ WHERE id = '0d3a0077-f065-4d6b-adbd-7bbb672efeda';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is D because Sertoli (sustentacular) cells physically surround all stages of the developing germ cells and secrete signaling molecules that promote sperm production and control whether germ cells live or die; removing them removes the support spermatogenesis depends on. The text states that "Surrounding all stages of the developing sperm cells are elongate, branching Sertoli cells" and that "Sertoli cells secrete signaling molecules that promote sperm production and can control whether germ cells live or die," so destroying these cells deprives the germ cells of both their physical scaffold and their pro-spermatogenic signals, halting sperm development. The toxin spares the Leydig (interstitial) cells, the basement membrane, and the lumen, so the failure traces specifically to the loss of Sertoli support rather than to a hormone, transport, or barrier defect. (Choice A) is wrong because testosterone is a "steroid hormone produced by Leydig cells," which the toxin spares, so androgen output would not collapse, and germ cells deprived of Sertoli support cannot finish spermatogenesis. (Choice B) is wrong because the lumen is left intact and Sertoli loss disrupts the formation of sperm, not merely their exit through the "straight tubules" and ducts. (Choice C) is wrong because although the "tight junctions between these sustentacular cells create the blood–testis barrier," losing the Sertoli cells also removes their nourishing and signaling support, so germ cells cannot mature normally. (Choice D) is correct because the Sertoli cells supply the physical envelopment, nourishment, and survival/pro-spermatogenic signals without which the germ cells cannot develop into sperm. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the defined roles of distinct seminiferous-tubule cell types to a selective-lesion scenario and predicting the downstream consequence rather than recalling a single stated fact.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Testosterone secretion collapses, but the surviving germ cells still complete spermatogenesis and release mature sperm.","label":"A"},{"text":"Sperm continue to form normally but become trapped because the tubule lumen through which they exit is now obstructed.","label":"B"},{"text":"The germ cells lose their blood–testis barrier yet still progress through meiosis and mature into normal sperm.","label":"C"},{"text":"Sperm development fails because the germ cells lose the surrounding cells that nourish them and secrete signals promoting sperm production.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '0d426176-6980-439d-9908-5778e6a91c50';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because in peptide-bond formation the amino group of one residue performs a nucleophilic attack at the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the activated carboxyl group of the other residue. Amino acids polymerize through a nucleophilic attack by the amino group of one amino acid at the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the carboxyl group of another; that carbonyl of the activated end serves as the electrophile, while the amine nitrogen serves as the nucleophile. Because the dipeptide is written N-to-C as Val-Ser, valine is the upstream (N-terminal) residue contributing its activated \(\alpha\)-carboxyl carbonyl carbon, and serine is the downstream residue contributing its \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen; the serine nitrogen (nucleophile, electron-pair donor) therefore attacks the valine carbonyl carbon (electrophile), releasing one \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\). (Choice A) reverses the roles: oxygen is not the attacking species here, and pairing it with the amino nitrogen as the target inverts both the nucleophile and the electrophile. (Choice B) correctly pairs valine's carbonyl with serine's nitrogen but reverses which is nucleophile and which is electrophile—it names the electrophilic carbon as the attacker. (Choice C) keeps the correct nucleophile (the amine nitrogen) but attacks the wrong carbonyl: serine's own carbonyl is the C-terminal carboxyl, so this would not link valine to serine and contradicts the N-to-C order. (Choice D) is correct: the serine \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen is the nucleophile and the valine carbonyl carbon is the electrophile. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the peptide-bond mechanism to a specific named sequence, using N-to-C directionality to assign which residue supplies the nucleophile and which supplies the electrophile.$px$ WHERE id = '0d7aa6b4-24ee-4646-9112-d5de9a4ec4b1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is D because 'indirect' refers to an interposed energy intermediate. In substrate-level phosphorylation a phosphoryl group passes directly from a high-energy donor to \(\text{ADP}\). In oxidative phosphorylation, by contrast, the free energy released as electrons fall down the chain is first captured in the form of a proton gradient (the proton-motive force) across the inner membrane; ATP synthase then uses that stored gradient — not any phosphorylated substrate — to make \(\text{ATP}\). The gradient is the go-between that makes the coupling indirect.

(Choice D) is correct: it identifies the transmembrane proton gradient as the interposed store of energy that powers phosphorylation of \(\text{ADP}\), which is exactly what distinguishes the indirect, gradient-mediated mechanism from a direct phosphoryl transfer.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it actually describes direct (substrate-level) phosphorylation and merely adds an oxygen requirement, so it does not capture the indirect, gradient-mediated nature of oxidative phosphorylation.
(Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{ADP}\) is not reduced by electrons; phosphorylation forms a phosphoanhydride bond from \(\text{ADP} + \text{P}_\text{i}\) and is not an electron-transfer (redox) event onto \(\text{ADP}\).
(Choice C) is incorrect: ATP synthase does not take a phosphate from \(\text{NADH}\); \(\text{NADH}\) donates electrons to the chain, no phosphate is ever transferred from \(\text{NADH}\) to \(\text{ADP}\), and denying any interposed carrier is the opposite of the indirect, gradient-mediated mechanism.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: you must contrast two mechanisms and reason about what the proton gradient accomplishes, recognizing that an interposed energy carrier is what makes oxidative phosphorylation indirect.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"A high-energy phosphorylated substrate hands its phosphate straight to ADP in one step, and the only added requirement is that molecular oxygen be present for the reaction","label":"A"},{"text":"Electrons from the chain are delivered directly onto ADP, and it is this reduction of ADP itself that creates the new phosphoanhydride bond of ATP","label":"B"},{"text":"ATP synthase pulls a phosphate straight off NADH and passes it to ADP, with no proton gradient or other energy carrier interposed in between","label":"C"},{"text":"Electron-transfer energy is first stored in a transmembrane proton gradient, and that gradient alone — not any phosphorylated substrate — drives the phosphorylation of ADP","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '0dca4eaf-b046-4cd7-82c4-2485510ab956';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This question targets periodic trends, specifically the cause of decreasing atomic radius left-to-right across a period. Moving from silicon (\(Z = 14\)) to chlorine (\(Z = 17\)), each added proton increases the nuclear charge while the new electrons fill the same \(n=3\) valence shell, \(3s^2 3p^x\). Because electrons in the same shell shield one another poorly, the screening constant \(S\) rises only slightly, so the effective nuclear charge \(Z_\text{eff} = Z - S\) climbs steadily. The stronger net pull draws the valence shell inward, giving chlorine the smaller radius. The constraint stated in the stem — same row, same number of shells — isolates nuclear charge as the operative variable.\n\n(Choice A) Both silicon and chlorine hold their valence electrons in the same principal level, \(n=3\); the highest occupied shell does not change across a period, so this misstates the electron configuration.\n\n(Choice B) Added electrons across a period enter the same valence shell rather than inner shells, so core shielding stays essentially constant; invoking greater shielding predicts the wrong direction, since rising \(Z_\text{eff}\) actually shrinks the atom.\n\n(Choice D) No new shell opens between silicon and chlorine — both end in \(n=3\) — and the modest increase in same-shell repulsion is outweighed by the larger nuclear charge, so this reverses the dominant effect.\n\nSkill 1$px$ WHERE id = '0e2dc5fa-1c1b-4303-85e1-c1cb63e941b0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is A because once the \(\alpha\)-amino group has been transaminated away, the remaining carbon skeleton is a plain \(\alpha\)-keto acid — here pyruvate — that the cell treats as fuel. Under an acute energy demand with oxaloacetate available to keep the TCA cycle turning, pyruvate is decarboxylated to acetyl-CoA, which condenses with oxaloacetate and is oxidized in the TCA cycle. That oxidation is precisely what generates the NADH and FADH2 that feed the electron transport chain, so this is the fate that directly captures the skeleton's energy as reducing equivalents.

(Choice A) is correct: the deaminated skeleton (pyruvate) is converted to acetyl-CoA and oxidized in the TCA cycle, producing the NADH and FADH2 that drive the electron transport chain.
(Choice B) is incorrect: re-aminating the skeleton back to alanine and storing it would undo the catabolic step just performed and capture no energy; during an energy demand the cell burns the skeleton rather than rebuilding the amino acid.
(Choice C) is incorrect: it is the nitrogen that was already removed which becomes free ammonia for urea; the carbon skeleton does not turn into ammonia, so this confuses the fate of the carbons with the fate of the nitrogen.
(Choice D) is incorrect: the carbon skeleton is a useful fuel, not waste, so the cell oxidizes it rather than dumping it unchanged into the blood; the waste product actually excreted is urea, made from the removed nitrogen.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason from the stated conditions — nitrogen already removed, oxaloacetate available, energy demanded — to identify the single metabolic route that converts the carbon skeleton into reducing equivalents, rather than restate a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '0e52acd9-937f-4391-96b5-550c60df4441';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because a binding protein works by physically bringing two molecules together, so a \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-binding sensor that can no longer grip \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) can no longer link the rise in \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to the target kinase, and the kinase stays inactive. (Choice A) is incorrect: this is the reversed-relationship misconception that the sensor holds the kinase off; in the scenario the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded sensor turns the kinase on, so abolishing the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) site removes activation, not an inhibitory brake. (Choice B) is correct: the sensor's job is to bring \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and the kinase together, so with a dead \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) site the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) signal is never coupled to the kinase and activation fails. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reflects the misconception that the small ligand acts directly on the enzyme and the binding protein is dispensable; the scenario shows the kinase turns on only through the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded sensor, not from \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) alone. (Choice D) is incorrect: this is the misconception that a binding protein chemically alters its partner; binding proteins associate noncovalently to juxtapose molecules and do not covalently modify the target, and the intact docking region would still bind, not destroy, the kinase. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the general function of binding proteins—that they act by bringing molecules together—and read out its consequence in the scenario.$px$ WHERE id = '0e8d17ce-f011-4f13-8bde-265345ced481';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A cave-dwelling salamander lineage and a cave-dwelling fish lineage last shared a common ancestor over 350 million years ago—an ancestor that had fully functional eyes. Both lineages independently colonized lightless caves, and today members of each group have smooth skin over the eye socket and rely on lateral-line and chemosensory organs to navigate. Reconstructing the ancestor confirms it was sighted, and the eye-reduction mutations differ between the two groups. Which macroevolutionary pattern best accounts for the shared blind phenotype in these two groups?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' Whether a shared trait reflects convergent or divergent evolution is decided by ancestry, not by how similar the organisms look: the deciding question is whether a common ancestor already had the trait (which the descendants then inherited) or whether the trait arose separately in each lineage. Here the reconstructed last common ancestor was fully sighted and the underlying eye-reduction mutations differ between the salamanders and the fish, so the blind phenotype was not inherited from a shared ancestor—it was built twice, independently, under the same selective pressure (permanent darkness). (Choice A) is correct: convergent evolution is the process by which distantly related groups that do NOT share the trait through recent common ancestry independently evolve to similar forms because of similar selective pressures; the sighted ancestor plus the different, independently acquired mutations are the signature of independent origin, so the matching blind phenotype is convergent. (Choice B) is incorrect: divergent evolution is the process by which groups sharing a recent common ancestor evolve in diverse directions from that common point, becoming more different over time; that is the opposite of what the data show, because these lineages started from a sighted ancestor and became more similar (both blind), not more different, and they are only distantly related. (Choice C) is incorrect: adaptive radiation is speciation in which a single ancestral species rapidly radiates into several new species filling distinct niches (e.g., Hawaiian honeycreepers with differently shaped beaks); the scenario involves two separate, already-distinct lineages arriving at the same phenotype, not one species multiplying into many niches. (Choice D) is incorrect: coevolution is reciprocal evolutionary change between two interacting groups that impose selection on each other (such as a plant and its pollinator); the salamanders and fish do not interact or select on one another—each responded independently to the physical environment of the cave. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that convergent evolution is defined by independent origin of a similar trait in lineages lacking recent shared ancestry for that trait, and use the ancestral state to distinguish it from divergence, radiation, and coevolution.$px$ WHERE id = '0f74a082-88c8-4236-b3da-c65c3a48f80e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because in Anfinsen's classic ribonuclease experiment, urea and \(\beta\)-mercaptoethanol unfold and reduce the enzyme, and once the reducing agent is removed under oxidizing conditions the disulfides reform and the denaturant is removed, the chain spontaneously refolds; allowed to reach its most stable set of bonds, it regains full activity. This demonstrated that all the information necessary and sufficient to direct the folding of a protein into its native state is contained in the primary sequence: after the \(\beta\)-mercaptoethanol was removed by dialysis so the disulfides could reform, and then the urea was removed, adding catalytic amounts of \(\beta\)-mercaptoethanol allowed the protein to recover essentially 100% of its initial activity. Because the native disulfides are tertiary-structure bonds that re-pair correctly as the chain refolds, function returns without any external instructions. (Choice A) is incorrect: denaturation disrupts the 3D (tertiary/quaternary) fold but does not break the peptide bonds, so the order and nature of amino acids in the primary sequence continue to determine the folding pattern throughout. (Choice B) is incorrect: the entire point is that no external template is needed — the fixed 3D structure is uniquely encoded in the primary structure (the amino acid sequence). (Choice C) is incorrect: ribonuclease is a single-chain protein, so quaternary subunit reassembly is irrelevant; quaternary structure applies only when separate protein chains interact to form dimers and larger assemblies. (Choice D) is correct: removing the reducing agent lets the cysteine thiols re-oxidize into the native disulfides (RSSR is the oxidized form of thiols), and refolding restores activity, demonstrating that the primary sequence encodes the native fold. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between primary sequence, disulfide reformation, and recovered function to predict the outcome of a defined unfolding-and-refolding protocol.$px$ WHERE id = '0f7f8bd3-5250-4f7c-9288-d11465c7a4b7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is B because Darwin reasoned that more offspring are produced than can survive, so resources for survival and reproduction are limited; the reproductive capacity of all organisms outstrips the resources available to sustain their numbers, so competition for those resources arises in each generation. Overproduction relative to resources is the tenet that sets up this struggle, and only the individuals with inherited variations that best compete will survive and have more offspring. (Choice A) Random change in allele frequencies describes genetic drift, which is simply the effect of chance rather than an advantage conferred by a genetically encoded trait — a non-selective process distinct from the resource competition overproduction creates. (Choice B) is correct: overproduction beyond what resources can support produces competition for those limited resources in each generation. (Choice C) Generation of new alleles by mutation is the ultimate source of new genetic variation, but it is the origin of the variation selection acts on, not the condition that overproduction establishes. (Choice D) Relative fitness is how an individual's reproductive contribution compares to that of the other organisms in the population, a downstream measure of selection's outcome rather than the condition overproduction directly creates. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall Darwin's tenet that the overproduction of offspring relative to limited resources produces competition within each generation.$px$ WHERE id = '0f89a000-7a90-43d5-b9e1-de468d420f11';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Embryogenesis & Development,' testing ectodermal cell-fate specification during neurulation/organogenesis. During organogenesis the ectoderm can give rise to two derivatives, the nervous system and the epidermis, and which cells take which path is set by growth-factor signaling: these growth factors direct some cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis cells, while the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate that rolls into the neural tube. The key inference is that neural fate is the default state of ectoderm; the signal is what diverts edge cells away from that default and toward epidermis. (Choice A) is correct: if growth-factor signaling is disrupted, the entire ectoderm differentiates into neural tissue, so removing the signal that pushes edge cells toward epidermis leaves more of the ectoderm following its default neural path. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the role of the signal—the growth factor induces epidermis, not neural tissue, so blocking it yields less epidermis, not more. (Choice C) is incorrect: ectoderm does not require this signal to become neural; neural differentiation is the default fate that proceeds in the signal's absence, so the tissue does not remain undifferentiated. (Choice D) is incorrect: this edge signal is precisely what partitions ectoderm into neural versus epidermal regions, so removing it shifts the proportions rather than leaving them unchanged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept that ectoderm has a default neural fate overridden by an inductive signal, then predict how removing that signal redistributes the two ectodermal derivatives.$px$ WHERE id = '0f8fa0dd-360d-4c05-8be3-9c82eb35c107';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because the phosphorylase described removes glucose one residue at a time from a terminus lacking a free anomeric carbon—that is, from a non-reducing end. Each linear chain segment in a branched polymer terminates in one such end, so the number of non-reducing ends scales with the number of branches. Because X has about three times as many branch points as Y for the same total number of residues, X presents many more non-reducing ends. With enzyme in excess, many phosphorylase molecules can dock on these many ends and release glucose in parallel, giving X a faster initial mobilization rate.

(Choice C) is correct: more branch points mean more non-reducing chain ends available at once, and since the enzyme acts only at those ends, more enzyme copies can work simultaneously, speeding release.

(Choice B) is incorrect: this phosphorylase acts on the α-1,4-linked residues of the linear segments, not on the α-1,6 branch linkages themselves (a separate debranching activity handles those), so having more α-1,6 bonds does not directly accelerate glucose release; the linkage type is also not the variable that sets the rate here.

(Choice A) is incorrect: a polysaccharide has only a single reducing end regardless of how many branches it has, and the enzyme described starts at ends without a free anomeric carbon (non-reducing ends), so branching does not multiply reducing ends or provide extra starting points of that kind.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the two polymers contain the same number of residues, so X is not smaller than Y; furthermore, generalized bond "accessibility" is not what limits this reaction—the number of valid terminal starting sites is.

Skill 2 note: answering requires reasoning from the enzyme's terminus specificity to the structural consequence of branching—recognizing that each added branch creates one more non-reducing end and that parallel action at many ends, not any change in bond chemistry, produces faster mobilization.$px$ WHERE id = '0ff6a919-c7f8-477f-8ea2-da397d34104a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is A because the mild copper(II) reagent oxidizes aldehydes but not ketones, so a ketose like fructose cannot be oxidized as-is. Under basic conditions the open-chain form of fructose undergoes keto–enol tautomerism: the C-2 carbonyl and the C-1 hydroxyl equilibrate through a common enediol intermediate, which can collapse toward C-1 to place the carbonyl there, converting the ketose into an aldose (glucose/mannose). That newly exposed aldehyde is what the reagent oxidizes. This rearrangement requires the sugar to be able to open its ring to the free carbonyl form. In sucrose, fructose is joined to glucose through its anomeric (C-2) carbon, so that carbon is locked as a full acetal; the fructose ring cannot open to a free carbonyl and therefore cannot tautomerize, which is exactly why sucrose is a nonreducing sugar. The contrast between free and sucrose-bound fructose thus pinpoints ring-opening plus tautomerization as the required step.

(Choice B) is incorrect: wrong because it violates the stated selectivity of the reagent, which does not touch ketones; a direct oxidation of the C-2 ketone to a carboxylic acid cannot occur, so "steric shielding" is not what silences the sucrose case.

(Choice A) is correct: it correctly routes the chemistry through base-catalyzed tautomerization of the open chain to an aldose, and it correctly explains the sucrose result by the anomeric carbon being tied up in the glycosidic bond so the ring cannot open.

(Choice C) is incorrect: wrong because it denies that any rearrangement is needed. If a free ketone were sufficient, the aldehyde-selective reagent would have no way to act on it; the reactivity depends on generating an aldehyde by tautomerization, not merely on the absence of a glycosidic bond.

(Choice D) is incorrect: wrong because it inverts the redox direction. The sugar is oxidized (it reduces Cu²⁺ to Cu⁺), and no reduction of the ketone to an alcohol occurs; a secondary alcohol is not the group that the reagent oxidizes here.

At the Skill 2 level, you cannot succeed by recalling that "fructose is a reducing sugar." You must reason from two constraints at once—an oxidant that spares ketones, and a fructose unit that reacts only when free—to infer that the reactive species is an aldehyde generated by base-catalyzed enediol tautomerism, a transformation that is impossible once the anomeric carbon is locked in an acetal.$px$ WHERE id = '101bb234-7e4e-4385-b36f-946c16d6965a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' A temperate bacteriophage such as λ can replicate its genome passively as an integrated prophage during the lysogenic cycle, but an environmental stressor can flip the switch: the prophage excises from the host chromosome and enters the lytic cycle, producing new virions that burst the cell. In the lysogenic cycle the viral genome is incorporated as a prophage and passed to daughter cells, and environmental stressors such as starvation or exposure to toxic chemicals can cause the prophage to excise and enter the lytic cycle. (Choice A) is correct: mitomycin C is a DNA-damaging chemical stressor, so it induces the prophage to excise and enter the lytic cycle; lytic replication lyses the bacteria (the falling optical density) and releases free progeny phage into the medium (the appearance of phage particles). (Choice B) is incorrect: induction does the opposite of stabilizing integration—the prophage leaves the chromosome—and the appearance of new phage particles cannot be explained by chemical toxicity acting on the cells alone, since a chemical does not manufacture virions. (Choice C) is incorrect: conjugation is a bacterium-to-bacterium transfer of DNA through a pilus, not a mechanism for releasing free phage, and it would not cause the culture-wide lysis reflected in the sharp drop in optical density. (Choice D) is incorrect: DNA damage induces the lysogenic-to-lytic switch rather than silently deleting the prophage, and a "cured" culture that kept dividing would show rising optical density and no free phage, the opposite of what was observed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of prophage induction to predict how a lysogenic culture responds to a DNA-damaging stressor, using the drop in cell number and appearance of free phage as evidence.$px$ WHERE id = '103107f8-e563-4981-af97-07f5623229fd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is A because the internodal distance in a healthy myelinated axon is set to an optimal value, and squeezing the nodes closer than that optimum lowers, rather than raises, the conduction speed. The text states that "the distance between nodes is the optimal distance to keep the membrane still depolarized above threshold at the next node," and explicitly that "If the nodes were any closer together, the speed of propagation would be slower" (A&P 22055-22059). Because the mutation merely shortens the spacing without preventing each node from reaching threshold, the impulse still travels node to node but loses the speed advantage of optimal spacing — it does not fail. The slowing reflects that closely packed nodes force more frequent regeneration of the action potential, the same factor that makes continuous (unmyelinated) conduction slow (A&P 22061-22063). (Choice A) is correct: closer-than-optimal nodes still reach threshold but propagate more slowly. (Choice B) is wrong because dissipation below threshold and conduction failure is the consequence of nodes spaced too FAR apart, where "that depolarization would have fallen off too much for voltage-gated Na+ channels to be activated at the next node" (A&P 22056-22058) — not too close. (Choice C) is wrong because more frequent regeneration is precisely what makes continuous conduction slow ("there are always voltage-gated Na+ channels opening," A&P 22062), so extra nodes slow propagation rather than speeding it. (Choice D) is wrong because, although myelin does enable saltatory conduction, the text establishes that internodal distance is an "optimal" value that directly affects speed (A&P 22054-22059), so spacing is not irrelevant. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the functional outcome of an experimental perturbation (a spacing mutation) by reasoning from the optimal-spacing passage rather than recalling a stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = '1044bdca-d055-4471-8de7-b55e2cc5b108';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because the medullary center modulates ventilation along two independent axes: the frequency of its rhythmic bursts sets the breathing rate, while the strength of each burst sets how forcefully the inspiratory muscles contract; stronger contractions expand the thoracic cavity more, drawing in a larger tidal volume per breath. (Choice B) is correct: increased burst strength drives more forceful diaphragm and external intercostal contraction, enlarging the thoracic cavity further per breath and raising tidal volume, which is the depth axis of ventilation distinct from rate. (Choice A) is incorrect: the number of breaths per minute is set by how often the center fires (burst frequency), not by burst strength, so this describes the rate axis rather than the depth change produced by stronger bursts. (Choice C) is incorrect: residual volume is the air that cannot be exhaled after a maximal expiration and is a fixed anatomical parameter not increased by stronger inspiratory contractions; exercise increases the volume actively moved, not trapped air. (Choice D) is incorrect: anatomical dead space is the fixed volume of the conducting airways and is not meaningfully enlarged by stronger inspiratory effort; deeper breathing actually improves the ratio of alveolar ventilation to dead space. This question tests the skill of reasoning that ventilation is controlled on two axes—rate and depth—rather than rate alone.$px$ WHERE id = '107aeedc-60d3-425b-908c-3a73c57787ad';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is B because Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium holds only under a specific set of assumptions, and of the three statements, only III (no migration in or out) is one of those required conditions. The Hardy-Weinberg principle assumes an infinitely large population with no mutation, no migration or emigration, and no selective pressure for or against any genotype. Because the locus is described by \(p + q = 1\) and the genotype frequencies \(p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1\), these frequencies stay constant from generation to generation only when no evolutionary force (no migration/gene flow, no mutation, no selection, no drift, random mating) perturbs the gene pool. Migration would add or remove alleles and shift \(p\) and \(q\), so the absence of migration in statement III is genuinely required. (Choice A) Statement I describes heterozygote advantage, a form of selective pressure favoring one genotype; selection is a force that violates equilibrium rather than maintaining it, so I is not a required condition. (Choice B) is correct: only statement III, the absence of migration and emigration, is among the listed Hardy-Weinberg conditions that must hold. (Choice C) pairs the true condition III with the false condition I, so it incorrectly treats heterozygote advantage as required. (Choice D) pairs III with II, but equilibrium does not require any particular dominance relationship at the locus—the equations hold for any allele frequencies regardless of which allele is dominant. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the set of assumptions that define Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium and distinguish a genuine equilibrium condition from evolutionary forces and irrelevant genetic facts.$px$ WHERE id = '109ecfb4-e971-494e-9598-629e34eeff00';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is D because the thyroid secretes mostly thyroxine (T4), which carries four iodine atoms and is relatively weak; peripheral deiodinase enzymes remove one iodine atom to form triiodothyronine (T3), the more metabolically active hormone that binds thyroid hormone receptors with far greater affinity. (Choice D) is correct: T3 (triiodothyronine) has three iodine atoms and is the product of removing one iodine from T4; it is several times more potent than T4 and is the principal form acting on target cells. (Choice A) is incorrect: Thyroxine (T4) is the four-iodine hormone actually secreted and described in the stem as weakly active — it is the substrate for the conversion, not its product. (Choice B) is incorrect: Thyroid-stimulating hormone is a pituitary glycoprotein that stimulates the thyroid to make and release thyroid hormones; it is not an iodinated hormone and is not made by deiodination in peripheral tissues. (Choice C) is incorrect: Calcitonin is a peptide hormone from the thyroid's parafollicular (C) cells that lowers blood calcium; it contains no iodine and is unrelated to metabolic rate or deiodinase activity. This item exercises the skill of tracing a hormone's peripheral activation pathway rather than matching vocabulary in the stem.$px$ WHERE id = '1180cc2b-7108-4b51-9a34-ab2c6d7bff25';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because bacteria reproduce by binary fission—a mechanism that does not use a microtubule mitotic spindle—so a drug that blocks spindle formation has no target to act on. In binary fission, the circular chromosome replicates from its origin and the copies are partitioned as the cell elongates; division is then driven by FtsZ, which "assembles into a Z ring on the cytoplasmic membrane" that "defines the division plane" and constricts the cell into two daughter cells (Microbiology, lines 14551-14559). This contractile-ring mechanism is fundamentally different from eukaryotic mitosis, in which a spindle of tubulin microtubules pulls sister chromatids apart; the text notes that "mitosis is a much more complex cellular process than binary fission" (Microbiology, lines 14559-14161). A spindle poison therefore cannot block a process that never builds a spindle in the first place. (Choice A) is wrong because prokaryotes lack a membrane-bound nucleus—"Prokaryotic DNA is in the cell's central part: the nucleoid" (Biology 2e, lines 5217-5218)—and, more importantly, the spindle inhibitor's failure is about the absence of a spindle, not about access to the DNA. (Choice B) reverses and mislabels the real mechanism: bacteria do not assemble a chromosome-pulling spindle of any kind, and the FtsZ ring constricts the membrane rather than dragging chromosomes along filaments, so this answer invents a spindle that does not exist. (Choice C) confuses transient arrest with true insensitivity; the cells are not pausing and resuming a spindle-driven division, because they never used a spindle—division continues uninterrupted via FtsZ. (Choice D) is correct because binary fission uses the FtsZ constriction ring, not a microtubule spindle, leaving the drug without a target. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the binary-fission-versus-mitosis distinction to predict that a spindle-targeting drug will not affect an organism whose division mechanism does not involve a spindle.$px$ WHERE id = '11b4e304-277f-4e32-bbda-8332723a5713';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is A because during the absolute refractory period the voltage-gated Na+ channels are in their inactivated state, so no stimulus of any strength can reopen them to trigger a second action potential. The channels' inactivation gates close at the peak of depolarization, and once inactivated they cannot be reopened for a brief interval—the absolute refractory period—so the neuron cannot fire again until they recover, regardless of stimulus strength. Stimulus strength is irrelevant here precisely because the gating, not the threshold, is the barrier. (Choice A) is correct because Na+ channel inactivation makes a second action potential impossible regardless of stimulus strength during the absolute period. (Choice B) is wrong because an elevated threshold that a stronger stimulus can overcome describes the relative, not the absolute, refractory period; during the absolute period no stimulus strength suffices. (Choice C) is wrong because reopening the Na+ channels depends on their return to the resting conformation, not simply on K+ channels closing. (Choice D) is wrong because recovery of excitability depends on Na+ channels returning from inactivation, not on the Na+/K+ pump restoring the ion gradient. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established mechanism that Na+ channel inactivation defines the absolute refractory period.$px$ WHERE id = '122d52ed-85f2-4d78-a8cc-0dfdcd4f74f4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is D because creatine phosphate ranks ABOVE ATP in phosphoryl-transfer potential, so a phosphate flows spontaneously (exergonically) from creatine phosphate onto ADP, regenerating ATP as fast as the muscle consumes it — a short-term ATP buffer that carries the muscle through the first few seconds before slower pathways (glycolysis, oxidative phosphorylation) engage. Creatine kinase catalyzes this exchange. When exertion stops and ATP is abundant again, the high [ATP]/[ADP] ratio (a very low reaction quotient Q for the forward direction) makes ΔG for the reverse reaction negative, driving it backward to re-form the creatine phosphate store for the next burst.

(Choice D) is correct: it captures both directions — rapid ATP regeneration during the burst (phosphate flowing from the higher-potential creatine phosphate to ADP) and store replenishment afterward when ATP is plentiful.

(Choice A) is incorrect: creatine phosphate does not directly power contraction — the contractile machinery hydrolyzes ATP, and creatine phosphate works by regenerating that ATP. Nor is the store rebuilt 'straight from creatine and Pi'; resynthesis runs the creatine kinase reaction backward using ATP.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it inverts the ranking. Creatine phosphate is HIGHER, not lower, than ATP in transfer potential, so phosphate flows from creatine phosphate TO ADP, not the reverse; the store is also rebuilt when ATP is plentiful, not after ATP is exhausted.

(Choice C) is incorrect: ATP, not creatine phosphate, remains the energy currency; creatine phosphate is a temporary reservoir that keeps ATP topped up, and ATP is continuously regenerated throughout the burst rather than left unreplenished until glycolysis.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: applying the transfer-potential gradient and the concentration dependence (ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q) of a reversible reaction to explain how a high-potential store buffers ATP during demand and recharges during rest.$px$ WHERE id = '1255f2a3-0d4b-44ae-9c14-b54b80308e58';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$The citric acid cycle's three primary regulatory control points are the reactions catalyzed by citrate synthase, isocitrate dehydrogenase, and the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex — all strongly exergonic, effectively irreversible steps. Steps such as the aconitase and fumarase reactions operate near equilibrium and are not major control points. Why does placing regulation at the irreversible steps give the cell the most effective control over cycle flux?$px$ WHERE id = '12899b62-3a60-4de0-8f9d-f2662274dda5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is A because conduction velocity depends on two separate determinants—axon diameter and resistance to current leak—and increasing either one speeds the impulse, so a large enough diameter advantage can compensate for the absence of myelin. The speed at which an action potential travels along an axon is influenced by both the diameter of the axon and the axon's resistance to current leak, with myelin acting as an insulator that reduces leak. Diameter matters because Na+-based depolarization spreads faster down a wide axon than down a narrow one, reflecting the lower internal resistance of a wide fiber. Because these are independent, additive factors, a wide unmyelinated fiber can have low enough internal resistance to match or beat a thin myelinated fiber whose saltatory advantage is offset by its small diameter. (Choice A) is correct because it treats diameter and myelination as separate determinants, either of which raises velocity, allowing diameter to compensate for missing myelin. (Choice B) is a tempting partial truth: myelin genuinely speeds conduction via saltatory jumping, but it ignores that diameter is a distinct, additive factor, so a thin myelinated axon need not beat a wide bare one. (Choice C) is wrong because the two factors do not cancel to a fixed velocity; both independently raise speed and do not trade off to a constant value. (Choice D) confuses the mechanisms, because an unmyelinated axon has no nodes of Ranvier and uses continuous conduction; its speed comes from low internal resistance, not from saltatory jumping. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using a measured outcome to infer that two independent mechanistic determinants of velocity—diameter and myelination—combine, rather than applying a single memorized rule.$px$ WHERE id = '12a1d0f9-4c02-4157-99f0-3217fc62b989';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' The Baltimore classification groups RNA viruses by how each makes mRNA: a positive-sense ssRNA genome "can serve directly as mRNA," but a negative-sense ssRNA genome has a sequence "complementary to the mRNA" and must first be copied into mRNA using a virus-encoded RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (lines 24801-24810). The task here is to reason from an experiment—naked genome transfection—to why one strategy launches infection and the other does not. (Choice A) is incorrect: reverse transcription into DNA is the strategy of retroviruses (Baltimore Group VI), not of ordinary negative-sense ssRNA viruses such as Virus Y; a negative-sense RNA virus replicates through RNA intermediates and never makes a DNA copy, so this misidentifies the missing step. (Choice B) is correct: because Virus X's genome is positive-sense, host ribosomes can immediately translate it into the viral proteins (including its polymerase) needed to begin replication, so the naked RNA alone is infectious; Virus Y's genome is the complement of mRNA and cannot be read by ribosomes, so it must be transcribed first by the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase that is normally packaged in the virion—an enzyme that was discarded when the proteins were stripped, leaving the naked RNA inert. (Choice C) is incorrect: both genomes were stripped of all proteins, so neither retains a capsid, and a bare genome's fate does not hinge on a capsid that is no longer present; the difference lies in polarity, not in protection from degradation. (Choice D) is incorrect: neither an ordinary positive- nor negative-sense ssRNA virus integrates into the host chromosome—integration is characteristic of retroviruses—so integration cannot be what distinguishes these two viruses. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that positive-sense RNA acts directly as mRNA while negative-sense RNA requires a packaged polymerase to interpret a novel transfection experiment.$px$ WHERE id = '12b57c52-ba0b-4f12-b0bc-2cf8f19d8511';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'

The answer is A because a good O2 transporter must load O2 in the lungs and then release a meaningful fraction of it in the tissues. Protein X is nearly fully loaded in the lungs (98% at ~100 mmHg) but drops to 60% at tissue pO2 (~30 mmHg), so it delivers roughly \(98\% - 60\% = 38\%\) of its capacity over the physiological range. This steep, cooperative (sigmoidal) response between lung and tissue pressures is exactly what makes hemoglobin an efficient shuttle. Protein Y stays almost saturated even at tissue pO2 (91%), releasing only ~6%, which is the high-affinity, hyperbolic behavior of myoglobin — a storage protein, not a transporter.

(Choice A) is correct: Protein X is the transporter. It is essentially saturated at lung pO2 yet gives up a large fraction of its O2 as pO2 falls to tissue levels; that large lung-to-tissue difference in saturation is what a circulating transporter must provide.

(Choice B) is incorrect: This reverses the roles. Protein Y's higher saturation at every pressure reflects higher affinity, which means it holds O2 too tightly to release it in the tissues. Higher affinity does not make a better transporter — it makes a better store, because the protein cannot let go of its cargo where it is needed.

(Choice C) is incorrect: It identifies the right protein but for the wrong reason. Protein X does not have low affinity "at all pressures" — it is ~98% saturated in the lungs, which requires that it bind O2 well when pO2 is high. Its usefulness comes from the change in affinity across the pressure range (cooperativity), not from binding weakly everywhere.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Reaching saturation at lower pressures (a high-affinity, hyperbolic curve, Protein Y) does help loading in the lungs, but net delivery depends on unloading in the tissues, not loading speed. Protein Y stays ~91% saturated at tissue pO2, so it barely releases any O2 and cannot serve as a transporter regardless of how readily it loads.

Skill 2: This item requires interpreting quantitative saturation data and reasoning about how the shape of an O2-binding curve maps onto physiological function — recognizing that transport efficiency is set by the difference in saturation between lung and tissue pO2, not by absolute affinity or loading behavior alone.$px$ WHERE id = '13677f2c-17d9-47f8-9b68-6187bfec9c39';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is C because the kidneys are an endocrine organ that secretes erythropoietin (EPO), the hormone that stimulates the bone marrow to produce erythrocytes; when the kidneys fail, EPO output falls, the marrow is under-stimulated, and few red-cell precursors form despite everything else being normal. The data are the discriminator: normal iron stores, a normal platelet count, and a structurally intact marrow rule out nutritional, structural, and destructive causes, leaving reduced endocrine drive from the failing organ. (Choice C) is correct: reduced renal secretion of erythropoietin removes the trophic signal to the marrow, so erythrocyte precursors are scarce even though raw materials and marrow architecture are intact. (Choice B) is incorrect: the anemia here is not a substrate-supply problem — serum iron and ferritin are both normal, so iron availability cannot be the limiting factor, and the kidney's endocrine role, not iron handling, is the relevant defect. (Choice A) is incorrect: the biopsy shows structurally normal, non-infiltrated marrow, so a destructive or fibrotic marrow process is excluded; the deficit is a missing hormonal signal, not a damaged production site. (Choice D) is incorrect: thrombopoietin drives platelet (megakaryocyte) production rather than red cells, and the platelet count is normal, so it is neither elevated nor the hormone whose deficiency would explain a low erythrocyte count. Skill 2: the item requires integrating an organ's endocrine function with laboratory data across several systems to isolate the hormonal cause of a disease state rather than recalling a single definition.$px$ WHERE id = '13d60aa2-11e1-49e1-8e8b-b7434ebe35c5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because a disaccharide gives a positive test only if at least one monosaccharide retains a free hemiacetal—an anomeric carbon still bearing an \(-OH\) that can reversibly open to the acyclic aldehyde and be oxidized. In linkage I, the glycosidic bond joins the anomeric C1 of one glucose to the anomeric C1 of the other, so BOTH anomeric carbons are locked into a full acetal. Neither ring can open to expose an aldehyde, and the disaccharide is non-reducing—it fails the test.

(Choice B) is correct: With both anomeric carbons committed to the bond, there is no free hemiacetal and no acyclic aldehyde can form; this is the same situation that makes sucrose (an anomeric-to-anomeric linkage) non-reducing.

(Choice A) is incorrect: A C1-to-C4 linkage (as in maltose or lactose) consumes only one anomeric carbon; the second glucose keeps its C1 as a free hemiacetal that can open to an aldehyde, so this disaccharide is reducing and tests positive.

(Choice C) is incorrect: A C1-to-C6 linkage (as in isomaltose) uses only the anomeric carbon of the first glucose; the C6 hydroxyl is a non-anomeric primary alcohol, so the second glucose retains a free anomeric hemiacetal and the sugar is reducing.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Here the anomeric C1 of one glucose bonds to the C2 hydroxyl of the other. Because C2 of glucose is a non-anomeric hydroxyl, the second glucose still has a free anomeric carbon at its own C1, leaving one open-able hemiacetal—so this disaccharide is reducing. (This is distinct from sucrose, where the 1,2 bond joins the anomeric C1 of glucose to the anomeric C2 of a ketose fructose, consuming both anomeric carbons.)

This is a Skill 2 item: rather than recalling that "sucrose is non-reducing," you must apply the rule that a free hemiacetal (an anomeric carbon still capable of ring-opening) is required for reduction, and evaluate each described linkage to determine whether both anomeric carbons have been consumed.$px$ WHERE id = '14654729-37b4-4e26-9a5f-657d4052888f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).' The cluster of observed events—nuclear envelope fragmenting, nucleolus disappearing, chromatids condensing into visible bodies, and centrosomes migrating apart with microtubules extending—identifies the cell as being in prophase, the first phase of mitosis. Near the end of prophase, after the nuclear membrane has disintegrated, spindle microtubules attach to the centromeres that adjoin the pairs of sister chromatids; this attachment occurs at the kinetochore, a protein structure on the centromere that is the point of attachment between the mitotic spindle and the sister chromatids. Only after this attachment can the chromatids be maneuvered into the metaphase plate. (Choice A) is correct: kinetochore–microtubule attachment is the transition event (late prophase/prometaphase) that must happen before the chromosomes can align midway between the poles at metaphase. (Choice B) is incorrect: cohesin degradation and separation of sister chromatids at the centromere define anaphase, which occurs only after alignment at metaphase, not next. (Choice C) is incorrect: DNA replication occurs during S phase of interphase and is already complete—the stem states the chromatids are duplicated—so it cannot be the next event. (Choice D) is incorrect: reassembly of nuclear envelopes around decondensing chromatin defines telophase, the final mitotic stage, which is far downstream of the observed prophase events. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of the ordered sequence of mitotic events to determine which step follows the observed prophase changes and enables metaphase alignment.$px$ WHERE id = '14d0199e-2b0e-4808-8ac2-16f5a9a7fa98';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Mechanisms of Development.' Determination is the point at which a cell's developmental fate becomes fixed; once a cell is determined, no other cell can take over the fate it was committed to. This distinction is illustrated by cleavage experiments: in embryos whose cells are already determined (determinate cleavage), removing a blastomere can result in an embryo that permanently lacks certain structures, whereas in embryos whose cells are not yet committed (indeterminate cleavage), removing individual blastomeres does not cause the loss of embryonic structures because adjacent cells compensate, and separated blastomeres can even form twins. (Choice A) is correct: a permanently missing, non-replaceable structure after removal is the signature of a cell whose fate was already fixed—the other cells cannot make what that cell alone was assigned to produce, so those cells were determined. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the logic. Embryo 1's cells recovered fully precisely because their fates were NOT yet fixed, letting neighbors reorganize and replace the missing cell; a fixed fate would have prevented that compensation. (Choice C) is incorrect: completing differentiation is the later acquisition of a specialized structure and function, not the commitment event, and a fully differentiated removed cell would not explain why the surrounding cells could rebuild normally—that outcome describes Embryo 1, whose cells were still uncommitted. (Choice D) is incorrect: ongoing cell division (cleavage) is mitotic partitioning and says nothing about whether fate is fixed; the differing experimental outcomes show the two embryos' cells were NOT equally committed, so shared division activity cannot establish equal determination. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of determination to interpret blastomere-removal results and infer which embryo's cells had fixed fates.$px$ WHERE id = '14d082a0-5487-47e7-930c-5e263b22bec2';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because the passages contracting in Patient 1 are bronchioles, which have smooth muscle in their walls but no cartilage, so contraction of that smooth muscle can dramatically constrict (or close) them; critically, the many bronchioles are arranged in parallel, so when diffuse constriction narrows all of them at once, the overall airway resistance rises steeply, whereas Patient 2's single fixed tracheal narrowing affects only one point in the airway and is limited by the trachea's supportive cartilage. (Choice C) is correct: bronchioles lack cartilage and rely on smooth muscle tone, and their parallel arrangement means simultaneous constriction of the whole population raises total resistance far more than one isolated fixed narrowing. (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the tissue identity — bronchioles contain smooth muscle and lack cartilage, not the other way around; the trachea is the structure supported by cartilage, so describing bronchioles as 'lined by cartilage' is anatomically backwards. (Choice B) is incorrect: while bronchioles do branch sequentially, the physiologically decisive point is that the enormous number of small airways function as parallel conduits; treating them as a simple in-series stack misrepresents why diffuse constriction is so damaging and would wrongly predict a single tracheal site could match it. (Choice D) is incorrect: bronchioles of the conducting zone are not the gas-exchange surface — that occurs across the alveoli — so their constriction impairs bulk airflow (ventilation), not diffusion across the membrane. This item exercises the skill of reasoning about how a structure's tissue composition and anatomical arrangement determine its physiological function.$px$ WHERE id = '14d5cd86-35fc-488b-b9e8-b55af3fd4d54';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses.' In a myelinated axon, voltage-gated Na+ channels are found only at the nodes of Ranvier; the internodal membrane beneath the myelin has no such channels, so it cannot regenerate the impulse. When Na+ enters at a node, the resulting charge spreads along the inside of the axon but progressively dissipates with distance, so the internode spacing must be set so that the membrane is still depolarized above threshold when the signal reaches the next node. (Choice A) is correct: between nodes the depolarization propagates passively (electrotonically) and decays with distance, so if it is to trigger the next node's Na+ channels it must still be above threshold on arrival — if the next node were positioned any farther along the axon, the depolarization would have decayed too much to activate the voltage-gated Na+ channels there. (Choice B) is incorrect: although saltatory conduction makes the impulse appear to jump from node to node, the internodal signal is not lossless — the depolarization actively dissipates as charge leaks, which is precisely why node spacing is constrained. (Choice C) is incorrect: continuous, channel-based regeneration along the whole membrane describes an unmyelinated axon; beneath the myelin there are no voltage-gated Na+ channels, so no regeneration occurs between nodes. (Choice D) is incorrect: myelin is an electrically passive insulator, not an amplifier; it reduces charge leakage but cannot generate or boost the signal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that internodal conduction is passive and decremental to explain why node spacing has an upper limit.$px$ WHERE id = '14fe7d36-6831-49a0-9342-a94a063bd094';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Edman degradation determines primary sequence by reacting phenylisothiocyanate with an uncharged, free N-terminal α-amino group, then cleaving that residue as a phenylthiohydantoin (PTH)-amino acid without disrupting the remaining peptide bonds; the cycle repeats to read the sequence from the N-terminus toward the C-terminus. The reaction therefore depends entirely on the N-terminal amine being available to attack the reagent. (Choice A) is correct: acetylation of the N-terminal α-amino group leaves no free amine for phenylisothiocyanate to react with, so no PTH-amino acid is generated in the first cycle at all, exactly the observed failure; Edman degradation cannot proceed when the N-terminus has been chemically modified, as by acetylation or by cyclization to pyroglutamic acid. (Choice B) is incorrect: the length limit (~50–60 residues) caps how many cycles can be read reliably because derivatization is incomplete over many rounds, but at 40 residues the chain is within range and, more importantly, length would never prevent the first residue from being released. (Choice C) is incorrect: a disulfide bond makes it hard to assign disulfide positions and can complicate sequencing across the linkage, but it does not block the free N-terminal amine, so a first PTH-amino acid would still be produced. (Choice D) is incorrect: Edman degradation proceeds from the N-terminus, so a modification of the C-terminal α-carboxyl group is irrelevant to the first cycle; this choice confuses the direction in which Edman chemistry reads the chain. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of N-terminal Edman chemistry to a novel experimental failure and infer which structural feature blocks the first sequencing cycle.$px$ WHERE id = '1528fdcc-1d62-4484-ac59-bab7f1d2b936';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the added strong base exceeds the buffer's acid reserve, so the buffer is destroyed and the leftover strong base, not the Henderson–Hasselbalch ratio, controls the \(\text{pH}\). The controlling step is the capacity check: \(\text{NaOH}\) reacts as \(\text{HA} + \text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{A}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O}\), but only 0.20 mol \(\text{HA}\) is present while 0.30 mol \(\text{OH}^-\) is added. The \(\text{OH}^-\) first consumes all 0.20 mol \(\text{HA}\) (producing 0.20 mol more \(\text{A}^-\), for 0.40 mol total), and \(0.30 - 0.20 = 0.10\ \text{mol}\) of strong \(\text{OH}^-\) remains in excess. Because Henderson–Hasselbalch requires both conjugate members to be present and the buffer no longer contains any \(\text{HA}\), the equation does not apply; the \(\text{pH}\) is fixed by the leftover strong base. In 1.00 L, \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.10\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pOH} = -\log(0.10) = 1.00\) and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 1.00 = 13.00\). The 0.40 mol \(\text{A}^-\) is the conjugate base of a weak acid and contributes negligibly to \([\text{OH}^-]\) against the 0.10 M strong-base background. (Choice A) \(4.90\) comes from applying Henderson–Hasselbalch blindly without the capacity check, taking \([\text{A}^-] = 0.20 + 0.30 = 0.50\) and \(\lvert 0.20 - 0.30\rvert = 0.10\) for \(\text{HA}\) to get \(4.20 + \log(5) = 4.90\); this treats a negative residual acid amount as if it were a real positive concentration. (Choice B) \(8.90\) arises from correctly noting that all \(\text{HA}\) is consumed but then discarding the 0.10 mol of leftover strong \(\text{OH}^-\), instead computing the \(\text{pH}\) of the 0.40 M \(\text{A}^-\) acting alone as a weak base via \(K_b = K_w/K_a\); this ignores that excess strong base swamps the weak-base hydrolysis. (Choice C) \(13.48\) results from recognizing that excess strong base sets the \(\text{pH}\) but using the full 0.30 mol of added \(\text{OH}^-\) as if none had been spent neutralizing the acid, giving \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.30\ \text{M}\) and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 0.52 = 13.48\); this omits the 0.20 mol consumed by \(\text{HA}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a stoichiometric capacity check with strong-base \(\text{pH}\) calculation and recognize when the Henderson–Hasselbalch principle no longer governs.$px$ WHERE id = '159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because the mucociliary escalator (mucus plus beating cilia) lines only the conducting airways and cannot reach the alveoli, which have no cilia; clearance of the small particles and microbes that penetrate this deep into the respiratory zone depends on phagocytosis by alveolar macrophages. With the macrophages depleted but every conducting-airway defense intact, only the deep-lung challenge is left uncleared. (Choice B) is correct: ~1 micron particles and bacteria are small enough to bypass upstream filtration and settle in the alveoli, where macrophages are the sole clearance mechanism — exactly the function that is missing. (Choice A) is incorrect: large ~15 micron particles are trapped by nasal hairs and the mucus of the upper airway, defenses that are explicitly intact, so their clearance is unaffected. (Choice C) is incorrect: bacteria in tracheal/bronchial mucus are swept upward by the intact mucociliary escalator toward the pharynx, independent of macrophages. (Choice D) is incorrect: upper-airway allergens are handled at the mucosal surface by intact mucus, secreted IgA, and lysozyme, not by alveolar macrophages. This question tests the skill of predicting the physiological consequence of a specific defect by mapping each defense mechanism to the airway region it protects.$px$ WHERE id = '15c4d49a-8e19-4bde-b2fd-acd68a85de44';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is B because an expansion of blood volume must be corrected by INCREASING the excretion of sodium and water, and atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is the only listed hormone that promotes natriuresis (sodium loss) and diuresis (water loss) rather than retention. (Choice B) is correct: When venous return and blood volume rise, the atrial myocardium releases ANP; ANP acts on the kidney to increase glomerular filtration and reduce sodium reabsorption, so more sodium and its accompanying water are excreted, lowering blood volume back toward normal — the physiologically appropriate correction for volume overload. (Choice A) is incorrect: Aldosterone from the adrenal cortex increases sodium (and thus water) REABSORPTION, which would worsen volume overload; it is secreted in response to low volume/low sodium, the opposite of this scenario. (Choice C) is incorrect: Antidiuretic hormone increases water REABSORPTION in the collecting ducts and is released when volume/osmolarity signals call for water conservation; retaining water is the wrong direction for a fluid-overloaded patient. (Choice D) is incorrect: Angiotensin II drives sodium and water REABSORPTION (directly in the proximal tubule and by stimulating aldosterone) and is part of the retention system activated by LOW volume, not high volume. This question exercises the skill of predicting the direction of a homeostatic renal response (excretion vs. retention) and then matching it to the correct hormone rather than relying on the source organ.$px$ WHERE id = '15d92e5c-0d8f-4711-82d3-4502a666b1e2';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is D because a leak channel lets an ion diffuse passively down its own concentration gradient, and the values show K+ at ~155 mM inside versus ~4 mM outside, so net diffusion through a K+ leak channel carries the ion out of the cell. The text states that \"in neurons, potassium ions are maintained at high concentrations within the cell while sodium ions are maintained at high concentrations outside of the cell,\" and that the cell \"possesses potassium and sodium leakage channels that allow the two cations to diffuse down their concentration gradient,\" with the result that \"potassium diffuses out of the cell.\" Because the intracellular concentration (~155 mM) greatly exceeds the extracellular concentration (~4 mM), the gradient points outward, and this continual K+ leak outward is precisely what helps establish the negative resting potential. (Choice A) is incorrect because it inverts the actual K+ gradient; the data show K+ is higher inside, not outside, so diffusion cannot drive it inward. (Choice B) is incorrect because the Na/K pump actually moves K+ into the cell against its gradient, but the stem restricts the prediction to passive diffusion through the leak channel, where the concentration gradient drives K+ outward. (Choice C) is incorrect because a concentration gradient for K+ clearly exists at rest—~155 mM inside versus ~4 mM outside—so the ion is not evenly distributed. (Choice D) is correct because K+ is far more concentrated inside, so diffusion through its leak channel drives it out of the cell. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires reading concrete concentration values from a scenario and predicting the resulting direction of passive ion movement rather than recalling a stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = '1612a525-4586-4ecf-bf63-3cc5d6e13190';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because in chronic respiratory acidosis the retained CO2 drives the bicarbonate equilibrium toward more H+, and the kidneys compensate slowly (over days) by reabsorbing filtered bicarbonate and generating new bicarbonate, raising plasma HCO3- so that the HCO3-/CO2 ratio—and thus pH—moves back toward normal despite the persistently high CO2. (Choice B) is correct: retaining and generating bicarbonate adds base that buffers the retained acid load and pulls pH upward, which is the defining slow metabolic (renal) offset to a respiratory disturbance. (Choice A) is incorrect: excreting bicarbonate would lower plasma HCO3- and worsen the acidosis rather than correct it; bicarbonate loss is the renal response to alkalosis, not acidosis. (Choice C) is incorrect: the kidneys do not excrete dissolved CO2 as such—CO2 clearance is a respiratory (lung) function, and renal compensation works by adjusting bicarbonate and H+ handling, not by exhaling or excreting CO2. (Choice D) is incorrect: renal compensation is slow, unfolding over days rather than minutes, and it operates precisely by altering bicarbonate reabsorption/generation and H+ secretion, so a bicarbonate-independent, near-instant mechanism mischaracterizes the timescale and pathway. This question tests the skill of reasoning about how the renal system provides a slow metabolic offset to a chronic respiratory acid-base disturbance.$px$ WHERE id = '162f7124-bcde-4035-8777-a43f0543b5b0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the phrenic nerve arises from spinal cord segments C3–C5 ("C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive"), so a complete transection at C2—above these segments—interrupts the descending drive from the brainstem to the phrenic motor neurons and paralyzes the diaphragm, making the patient ventilator-dependent; a C6 lesion lies below C3–C5 and therefore spares the phrenic outflow and diaphragmatic breathing. (Choice A) is correct: a C2 lesion is rostral to the C3–C5 phrenic motor pool, cutting the pathway carrying the medullary rhythm to those motor neurons, so the diaphragm loses its drive and cannot contract. (Choice B) is incorrect because a C6 lesion is below, not above, the C3–C5 origin; it leaves the phrenic outflow intact, so diaphragmatic breathing is preserved in Patient 2. (Choice C) is incorrect because the medullary respiratory center lies in the brainstem, well above the spinal cord; a cervical cord lesion at C2 leaves the medulla intact and rhythm generation continues—the problem is that this rhythm can no longer reach the phrenic neurons. (Choice D) is incorrect because a C6 lesion is caudal to the C3–C5 segments and does not sever the phrenic motor neurons themselves; those cell bodies and their outflow remain intact and functional. This question tests the skill of reasoning from spinal anatomy to predict how the level of a cord lesion determines whether diaphragmatic ventilation is preserved or lost.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"Patient 1, because a lesion at C2 lies above the C3–C5 origin of the phrenic nerve and cuts off descending drive to the phrenic motor neurons","label":"A"},{"text":"Patient 2, because a lesion at C6 lies above the C3–C5 origin of the phrenic nerve and cuts off descending drive to the phrenic motor neurons","label":"B"},{"text":"Patient 1, because a lesion at C2 destroys the medullary respiratory center that generates the breathing rhythm","label":"C"},{"text":"Patient 2, because a lesion at C6 severs the phrenic motor neurons themselves within the C3–C5 cord segments","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '16a0173e-066c-4d70-a1a9-496a0d744491';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because two facts together explain why only the liver defends blood glucose. First, glucagon's primary target is the hepatocyte, not the myocyte, so glucagon drives glycogenolysis chiefly in the liver. Second, the liver contains glucose-6-phosphatase and can dephosphorylate glucose-6-phosphate to free glucose for export, whereas skeletal muscle lacks this enzyme — so even when muscle glycogen is broken down, the glucose-6-phosphate stays trapped inside the cell and is oxidized internally rather than released to the blood.

(Choice A) is incorrect: muscle glycogen is the same molecule as liver glycogen and is readily cleaved by muscle glycogen phosphorylase. The barrier is the missing glucose-6-phosphatase and muscle's lack of glucagon responsiveness, not any difference in the glycogen itself.

(Choice B) is incorrect: skeletal muscle is not a significant glucagon target (it lacks glucagon receptors). Muscle-derived glucose stays internal because muscle has no glucose-6-phosphatase, not because a strong glucagon signal is being consumed by contraction. Note also that muscle glycogenolysis liberates glucose-6-phosphate, not free glucose.

(Choice D) is correct: the hepatocyte is glucagon's primary target and the liver alone has glucose-6-phosphatase to release free glucose, while muscle lacks that enzyme and cannot export glucose derived from its glycogen.

(Choice C) is incorrect on two counts. Muscle is not a strong glucagon target — the hepatocyte is — so glucagon does not drive muscle glycogenolysis the way it drives the liver's. Just as importantly, phosphorylated glucose-6-phosphate is not shuttled out of the myocyte to the liver; charged phosphosugars do not cross the membrane, and muscle instead keeps its glucose-6-phosphate for in-situ glycolysis. The carbon that muscle does hand to the liver travels as lactate (Cori cycle) or alanine (glucose-alanine cycle), not as glucose-6-phosphate.

This question rewards Skill 2 (reasoning): you had to combine target specificity with a tissue-specific enzyme difference to explain why only the liver defends blood glucose.$px$ WHERE id = '172761b4-d053-4b3c-8704-5dc05ca12386';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the rate of O2 diffusion across the respiratory membrane depends on the partial-pressure gradient between alveolar air and capillary blood; at altitude the reduced atmospheric (and therefore alveolar) PO2 falls to about 60 mmHg, so the gradient driving O2 into venous blood (still ~40 mmHg) shrinks from ~64 mmHg to ~20 mmHg, slowing uptake even though the membrane itself is unchanged. (Choice A) is correct: with a normal membrane, the only variable that changed is the alveolar PO2, and a smaller alveolar-to-blood PO2 difference means a weaker driving force and slower diffusion, producing hypoxemia. (Choice B) is incorrect: atmospheric pressure does not deposit tissue or thicken the respiratory membrane; the stem specifies membrane thickness is normal, so diffusion distance is unchanged. (Choice C) is incorrect: the solubility of O2 in the fluid lining is a fixed physical property of the gas and liquid and does not fall when PO2 drops; a lower PO2 lowers the amount dissolved at equilibrium, but that reflects the gradient effect, not a change in solubility. (Choice D) is incorrect: alveolar surface area is an anatomical feature of the lung and is not reduced by lower barometric pressure; the stem states surface area is normal. This item tests the skill of reasoning about how a change in one determinant of gas diffusion—the partial-pressure gradient—alters the rate of alveolar gas exchange.$px$ WHERE id = '1767a403-3779-4899-83e1-c9ac9c622ca6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC).' Oxygen is assigned an oxidation number of \(-2\) in most compounds, but the guidelines note an important exception: in peroxides oxygen is \(-1\). Because the identity "peroxide" is given but the oxygen value is not, you must combine the sodium value with the rule that oxidation numbers in a neutral compound sum to zero rather than reflexively applying the default oxygen rule. (Choice B) is correct: sodium, a Group 1 metal, is \(+1\), so the two sodium atoms contribute \(2 \times (+1) = +2\). For the neutral compound the atoms must sum to zero, so the two oxygen atoms must total \(-2\); letting each oxygen be \(x\), \(2(+1) + 2x = 0\) gives \(x = -1\), the value characteristic of a peroxide. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(-2\) comes from applying oxygen's default oxidation number without recognizing that \(\text{Na}_2\text{O}_2\) is a peroxide; that value would make the atoms sum to \(2(+1) + 2(-2) = -2\), which fails to equal the required zero charge of a neutral compound. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(0\) results from over-applying the idea that a bond between two identical atoms (the O–O bond) is split evenly and contributes zero, but oxidation number must also account for the electrons oxygen pulls from the two electropositive sodium atoms, which makes each oxygen negative. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(+1\) reverses the sign of the correct magnitude, mistakenly assigning oxygen the same \(+1\) value as the sodium cations even though oxygen is far more electronegative and must therefore carry a negative oxidation number. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the oxidation-number guidelines to a peroxide, recognizing that the default oxygen value does not hold and solving the neutral-sum constraint to obtain the correct value.$px$ WHERE id = '17968c2c-95bf-4372-b242-0280d9340218';

COMMIT;
